window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2024-12-1", "source": "2024年12月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence “To increase the likelihood of success, one should set realistic goals and work persistently towards them.\" You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.You should copy the sentence given in quotes at the beginning of your essay.", "sample": "To increase the likelihood of success, one should set realistic goals and work persistently towards them. This\nstatement reveals a simple truth: ambition alone means little unless it is anchored to attainable targets and\nsustained effort.\nA realistic goal keeps us moving instead of despairing. If a beginner runner vows to finish a marathon next\nweek, failure is almost certain, and disappointment soon kills motivation. However, by aiming first at three\nkilometres, then five, and finally the full distance, the runner sets goals within reach and steadily raises the bar.\nEach small victory fuels the next.\nYet reasonable goals mean nothing without persistence. My cousin, who dreamed of passing CET-6, did not\nexpect overnight fluency. She fixed a modest daily plan of thirty new words and one reading passage, and she\nstuck to it for months. As a result, she cleared the exam comfortably.\nIn this way, pairing sensible targets with steady work transforms a distant dream into a series of achievable\nsteps. That is the surest road to success."}, "translation": {"prompt": "北斗(Beidou)卫星导航系统的成功研制是中国自改革开放以来取得的一项重大科技成就。研发人员经过不懈努力,攻克了一系列技术难题,北斗系统最终实现了全球覆盖和高精度定位,使中国成为世界上少数几个独立拥有全球卫星导航系统的国家之一。北斗系统已广泛应用于交通运输、灾害救援、天气预报、公共安全等诸多领域。北斗系统现在已经在国际上得到广泛认可,开始为越来越多的国家和地区提供优质服务。", "sample": "The successful development of the Beidou Navigation Satellite System is a major scientific and technological achievement made by China since the reform and opening-up. Through unremitting efforts, the developers overcame a series of technical problems, and the Beidou system has finally achieved global coverage and high-precision positioning, making China one of the few countries in the world that independently possess a global satellite navigation system. The system has been widely applied in many fields such as transportation, disaster relief, weather forecasting and public safety. Today, it has also been widely recognized internationally and has begun to provide high-quality services for an increasing number of countries and regions."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2024-12-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "With the rapid progress in the economy, it is natural that people's ideology is bound for change. Being  __26__  is no longer a virtue to some people, and they are not alone in holding this view. Each might have different viewpoints on how to lead one's lifestyle: some prefer simplicity, some love luxuries, some spend  __27__  , some accumulate as much as they can like a country mouse. As I was born poor and in the countryside, the simple rural life seldom loses its  __28__  to me, but as my station changes, I have an opportunity to  __29__  with the newly rich, an experience which has revolutionized my view of lifestyle.\nIt is evil or sinful to spend little and to save much, an idea only recently being revealed to me. Actually the whole world is in a  __30__  to borrow and to spend, with the USA leading the trend. The USA is still the richest and most powerful country in the world, but the  __31__  is that they borrow money from us. Though a  __32__  of economics, at least I know that investing with borrowed money will turn the borrower into the  __33__  rather than the decent and kind lender. An apparent case is real estate. Those who are  __34__  and have got loans from the bank become relatively rich by selling their purchased home at a price several folds higher. Even education is not immune from this theory. Many of those poor parents who have the vision to send their children to college have benefited from this investment, even though they have to  __35__  a living. It is reasonable to conclude that spending is praiseworthy, supposing it is not beyond your means.", "matchIntro": "Are Forgotten Crops the Future of Food?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "On a small fruit farm near the Straits of Malacca, Lim Kok Ann is down to just one tree growing kedondong, a crunchy, sour berry that Malaysians mostly use in pickles(腌菜) and salads. \"It's not very well-known,\" says the 45-year-old, who is instead focusing on longan(龙眼) berries and pineapples, which have bigger markets. \"We have to grow what is profitable,\" he says."}, {"label": "B", "text": "But less than an hour away in the Malaysian countryside, inside three giant, silver domes, scientists are trying to change the future of food. They're pushing the boundaries of what humans eat by growing and processing so-called \"alternative\" crops—such as kedondong. At the headquarters of global research centre Crops For the Future (CFF) this particular under-used fruit has been turned into a sugar-free juice, high in vitamin C and getting top marks in sensory evaluations. \"Anything you see here is a forgotten crop,\" says Sayed Azam-Ali of the abundant plants weaving through the gardens of CFF outside Malaysia's capital Kuala Lumpur."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Prof Azam-Ali explains that just four crops—wheat, maize, rice and soybean—provide two-thirds of the world's food supply. \"We're dependent on these four,\" he says. \"But actually there're 7,000 crops we've been farming for thousands of years. We ignore all of those.\" Researchers are trying to unlock the potential of these ignored crops—plants they describe as forgotten, under-used or \"alternative\" as they are displaced by increasingly uniform diets fuelled by processed ingredients from the major crops."}, {"label": "D", "text": "It's a timely quest. The food sector is already responsible for nearly a third of global greenhouse gas emissions. By 2050 it estimates the world must produce 50% more food to feed the projected global population of 10 billion. Meeting this demand without contributing to climate change calls for urgent solutions."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Forgotten crops hold key answers. By investing in neglected local plants, countries can reduce their reliance on imported crops and their carbon-heavy supply chains. Bringing back the variety of crops humans once ate also boosts food security at a time warming climates threaten existing crops. On top of that forgotten crops are among the most climate-resilient(具有气候韧性的) and nutritious, argues Azam-Ali. His summary is plain: \"Dietary diversification is critical to the future of humanity.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "Food security experts agree. \"There is no food insecurity in the world, there is food ignorance,\" says Cecilia Tortajada, a senior research fellow at the Institute of Water Policy at the National University of Singapore. \"Whenever we have native crops we tend to disregard them as if they were not valuable but they are,\" she adds."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Azam-Ali knows that scepticism firsthand. He came across alternative crops in the 1980s through the work of women farmers he met in Niger. The then-PhD student remembers marvelling at the crops they grew in their backyards, without the benefits of technology, to feed their families when the big crops failed. He saw a tremendous opportunity to build alternative food systems. But \"the resistance was enormous,\" he recalls."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Undeterred, he ploughed on. Project after project helped prove these crops were viable in different environments as alternatives to the staple ones. But the question of whether these crops would be marketable remained. \"That's the critical thing,\" he says. In one of the centre's domes, food technologist Tan Xinlin uses powdered moringa(辣木) leaves in place of some wheat flour to bake a cake lower in gluten(谷胶) and higher in nutrients. Tan's job is to create recipes with these still-unfamiliar ingredients that will appeal to both local and international tastes. In recent years she has used some of the forgotten crops grown at CFF, such as moringa and bambara groundnut, to make everything from instant soup to Indian snacks. \"I try to modernise forgotten crops instead of using old recipes.\" says Tan, who is also a trained chef. It's a strategy to appeal to the world's growing middle classes who are increasingly turning to the fast and processed food industries. It's also a way to help counter perceptions of local crops as \"old or poor people's food\" or as inferior \"women's crops\", adds Tan."}, {"label": "I", "text": "The roots of these connotations about local foods can run deep. The bambara groundnut, a protein-rich native crop of sub-Saharan Africa that is also grown in parts of southeast Asia, can trace its marginalisation to colonial rule. \"African women who grew bambara groundnut were actually punished for growing it,\" says Azam-Ali. \"Colonial powers said you can't grow that because there's no oil. We can't get a market for it.\" But today the bambara murukku is one of CFF's best reviewed foods and they are aiming to get it into grocery stores, pointing to the success of crops like quinoa to potential investors. Some 30 years ago, quinoa was virtually unheard of outside its native mountains in Bolivia and Peru. Today the nutritious grain is found on the menus of lavish restaurants across the world."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Measuring crops by nutrition instead of yield is at the heart of the forgotten foods enterprise. Ever since the \"green revolution\" of the 1960s, high-yielding crops have dominated modern agriculture. That was in part a crucial response to devastating famines at a time when the world needed to increase its food supply. Today \"nutrition is becoming a time bomb\", says Azam-Ali, as growing carbon dioxide levels strip crops of their minerals. Instead of bio-fortifying major crops we should be investing in those forgotten crops that are already more nutritious, he asserts."}, {"label": "K", "text": "In the bowels of CFF's third dome, lab manager Gomathy Sethuraman opens a window into the centre's \"crown jewels\", revealing vines of winged beans growing under a bright yellow light. It's one of multiple chambers where scientists are studying the impact of higher temperatures and carbon dioxide levels on the nutritional make-up of alternative crops. This research is \"the game changer\", says Azam-Ali, ensuring that \"future crops\" are also the healthiest ones in warmer climates."}, {"label": "L", "text": "There is a growing global momentum around forgotten foods, says Danielle Nierenberg, president of Food Tank, a US-based think tank. Other than CFF, which bills itself as the world's first research centre dedicated solely to under-utilised crops, there are other key groups championing agricultural diversity including Crop Trust, Slow Food, Icrisat and Bioversity International. Add to that more middle-income consumers searching for nutritious foods and others eager to try the unprocessed foods their grandparents once ate, she says."}, {"label": "M", "text": "But the rising interest in forgotten foods in some quarters is overtaken by the global spread of Western-style diets heavy in sugar, fat and processed foods in others."}, {"label": "N", "text": "A key obstacle to promoting fading local crops in Malaysia, for example, is \"the obsession with imported products\", says Jenifer Kuah, co-founder of a restaurant that champions locally-sourced food in an affluent suburb of Kuala Lumpur. Customers at Sitka, regarded as a pioneer in the country's small farm-to-table dining scene, still seek foreign ingredients as a \"status symbol\", she says."}, {"label": "O", "text": "The argument for forgotten foods feels intuitive. Some analysts say it is in fact inevitable. \"Climate change is going to mean almost certainly tastes are going to be forced to change,\" says Tim Lang, professor of food policy at City, University of London. We \"have to get used to eating other crops\" as yields of staple crops fall, he says."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Imagine you're an alien sent to Earth to document the behaviour of the mammals inhabiting the planet. You stumble into a movie theatre that's showing the latest Hollywood horror film.\nSeveral dozen humans are gathered together in a dark, undecorated room. They're all staring at a rectangular area on which patterns of light change rapidly.\nThey are clearly in a state of high arousal. Their heart rate is elevated, they occasionally glance around nervously, and they sometimes jump collectively in their seats, and emit high-pitched warning calls.\nEventually, the lights come up and the rectangular screen goes black. The humans stand up and leave the room, chatting and laughing, and showing signs of pleasure.\nWhy do these humans voluntarily expose themselves to what appears to be a deeply unpleasant experience? And why do they react so strongly to those patterns of light on a screen?\nI am fascinated with the paradox of horror—the strange fact that many people seek out scary entertainment.\nI think the answer to the puzzle lies in human nature.\nMy research suggests that we humans evolved to find pleasure in situations that allow us to experience negative emotions in a safe context. You can see these elements of horror in children's games. Take hide-and-seek for example, which is a simulation of a predator-prey interaction. The kid hides and the adult pretends to be a predator, searching for the child while howling like a dangerous beast.\nThis simulation gives the child crucial information about how to avoid becoming prey, and children tend to find that kind of activity deeply satisfying, presumably because it gives them a safe experience of a potentially catastrophic scenario.\nThey find it pleasurable, and pleasure is evolution's way of motivating us toward adaptive behaviour.\nHorror is pleasurable to many people because it lets us play with negative emotions and develop coping strategies. We learn what it feels like to be truly afraid, and we learn how to handle negative emotion.\nHow, then, does horror work? My research suggests that horror works by exploiting an ancient set of biological defence mechanisms—an evolved fear system, which we share with other animals. But humans are uniquely imaginative, and we use our evolved imagination to travel into virtual worlds that are full of danger.\nThere are good reasons for watching a horror film, even if you're not a loyal horror fan. If you make it through the film in one piece, you'll probably experience a strong sense of mastery, a sense that you were able to make it through an appalling experience. Anyway, watching a horror film makes you better at handling your own fear, and who knows when that will become critically relevant?", "47": "Imagine you're an alien sent to Earth to document the behaviour of the mammals inhabiting the planet. You stumble into a movie theatre that's showing the latest Hollywood horror film.\nSeveral dozen humans are gathered together in a dark, undecorated room. They're all staring at a rectangular area on which patterns of light change rapidly.\nThey are clearly in a state of high arousal. Their heart rate is elevated, they occasionally glance around nervously, and they sometimes jump collectively in their seats, and emit high-pitched warning calls.\nEventually, the lights come up and the rectangular screen goes black. The humans stand up and leave the room, chatting and laughing, and showing signs of pleasure.\nWhy do these humans voluntarily expose themselves to what appears to be a deeply unpleasant experience? And why do they react so strongly to those patterns of light on a screen?\nI am fascinated with the paradox of horror—the strange fact that many people seek out scary entertainment.\nI think the answer to the puzzle lies in human nature.\nMy research suggests that we humans evolved to find pleasure in situations that allow us to experience negative emotions in a safe context. You can see these elements of horror in children's games. Take hide-and-seek for example, which is a simulation of a predator-prey interaction. The kid hides and the adult pretends to be a predator, searching for the child while howling like a dangerous beast.\nThis simulation gives the child crucial information about how to avoid becoming prey, and children tend to find that kind of activity deeply satisfying, presumably because it gives them a safe experience of a potentially catastrophic scenario.\nThey find it pleasurable, and pleasure is evolution's way of motivating us toward adaptive behaviour.\nHorror is pleasurable to many people because it lets us play with negative emotions and develop coping strategies. We learn what it feels like to be truly afraid, and we learn how to handle negative emotion.\nHow, then, does horror work? My research suggests that horror works by exploiting an ancient set of biological defence mechanisms—an evolved fear system, which we share with other animals. But humans are uniquely imaginative, and we use our evolved imagination to travel into virtual worlds that are full of danger.\nThere are good reasons for watching a horror film, even if you're not a loyal horror fan. If you make it through the film in one piece, you'll probably experience a strong sense of mastery, a sense that you were able to make it through an appalling experience. Anyway, watching a horror film makes you better at handling your own fear, and who knows when that will become critically relevant?", "48": "Imagine you're an alien sent to Earth to document the behaviour of the mammals inhabiting the planet. You stumble into a movie theatre that's showing the latest Hollywood horror film.\nSeveral dozen humans are gathered together in a dark, undecorated room. They're all staring at a rectangular area on which patterns of light change rapidly.\nThey are clearly in a state of high arousal. Their heart rate is elevated, they occasionally glance around nervously, and they sometimes jump collectively in their seats, and emit high-pitched warning calls.\nEventually, the lights come up and the rectangular screen goes black. The humans stand up and leave the room, chatting and laughing, and showing signs of pleasure.\nWhy do these humans voluntarily expose themselves to what appears to be a deeply unpleasant experience? And why do they react so strongly to those patterns of light on a screen?\nI am fascinated with the paradox of horror—the strange fact that many people seek out scary entertainment.\nI think the answer to the puzzle lies in human nature.\nMy research suggests that we humans evolved to find pleasure in situations that allow us to experience negative emotions in a safe context. You can see these elements of horror in children's games. Take hide-and-seek for example, which is a simulation of a predator-prey interaction. The kid hides and the adult pretends to be a predator, searching for the child while howling like a dangerous beast.\nThis simulation gives the child crucial information about how to avoid becoming prey, and children tend to find that kind of activity deeply satisfying, presumably because it gives them a safe experience of a potentially catastrophic scenario.\nThey find it pleasurable, and pleasure is evolution's way of motivating us toward adaptive behaviour.\nHorror is pleasurable to many people because it lets us play with negative emotions and develop coping strategies. We learn what it feels like to be truly afraid, and we learn how to handle negative emotion.\nHow, then, does horror work? My research suggests that horror works by exploiting an ancient set of biological defence mechanisms—an evolved fear system, which we share with other animals. But humans are uniquely imaginative, and we use our evolved imagination to travel into virtual worlds that are full of danger.\nThere are good reasons for watching a horror film, even if you're not a loyal horror fan. If you make it through the film in one piece, you'll probably experience a strong sense of mastery, a sense that you were able to make it through an appalling experience. Anyway, watching a horror film makes you better at handling your own fear, and who knows when that will become critically relevant?", "49": "Imagine you're an alien sent to Earth to document the behaviour of the mammals inhabiting the planet. You stumble into a movie theatre that's showing the latest Hollywood horror film.\nSeveral dozen humans are gathered together in a dark, undecorated room. They're all staring at a rectangular area on which patterns of light change rapidly.\nThey are clearly in a state of high arousal. Their heart rate is elevated, they occasionally glance around nervously, and they sometimes jump collectively in their seats, and emit high-pitched warning calls.\nEventually, the lights come up and the rectangular screen goes black. The humans stand up and leave the room, chatting and laughing, and showing signs of pleasure.\nWhy do these humans voluntarily expose themselves to what appears to be a deeply unpleasant experience? And why do they react so strongly to those patterns of light on a screen?\nI am fascinated with the paradox of horror—the strange fact that many people seek out scary entertainment.\nI think the answer to the puzzle lies in human nature.\nMy research suggests that we humans evolved to find pleasure in situations that allow us to experience negative emotions in a safe context. You can see these elements of horror in children's games. Take hide-and-seek for example, which is a simulation of a predator-prey interaction. The kid hides and the adult pretends to be a predator, searching for the child while howling like a dangerous beast.\nThis simulation gives the child crucial information about how to avoid becoming prey, and children tend to find that kind of activity deeply satisfying, presumably because it gives them a safe experience of a potentially catastrophic scenario.\nThey find it pleasurable, and pleasure is evolution's way of motivating us toward adaptive behaviour.\nHorror is pleasurable to many people because it lets us play with negative emotions and develop coping strategies. We learn what it feels like to be truly afraid, and we learn how to handle negative emotion.\nHow, then, does horror work? My research suggests that horror works by exploiting an ancient set of biological defence mechanisms—an evolved fear system, which we share with other animals. But humans are uniquely imaginative, and we use our evolved imagination to travel into virtual worlds that are full of danger.\nThere are good reasons for watching a horror film, even if you're not a loyal horror fan. If you make it through the film in one piece, you'll probably experience a strong sense of mastery, a sense that you were able to make it through an appalling experience. Anyway, watching a horror film makes you better at handling your own fear, and who knows when that will become critically relevant?", "50": "Imagine you're an alien sent to Earth to document the behaviour of the mammals inhabiting the planet. You stumble into a movie theatre that's showing the latest Hollywood horror film.\nSeveral dozen humans are gathered together in a dark, undecorated room. They're all staring at a rectangular area on which patterns of light change rapidly.\nThey are clearly in a state of high arousal. Their heart rate is elevated, they occasionally glance around nervously, and they sometimes jump collectively in their seats, and emit high-pitched warning calls.\nEventually, the lights come up and the rectangular screen goes black. The humans stand up and leave the room, chatting and laughing, and showing signs of pleasure.\nWhy do these humans voluntarily expose themselves to what appears to be a deeply unpleasant experience? And why do they react so strongly to those patterns of light on a screen?\nI am fascinated with the paradox of horror—the strange fact that many people seek out scary entertainment.\nI think the answer to the puzzle lies in human nature.\nMy research suggests that we humans evolved to find pleasure in situations that allow us to experience negative emotions in a safe context. You can see these elements of horror in children's games. Take hide-and-seek for example, which is a simulation of a predator-prey interaction. The kid hides and the adult pretends to be a predator, searching for the child while howling like a dangerous beast.\nThis simulation gives the child crucial information about how to avoid becoming prey, and children tend to find that kind of activity deeply satisfying, presumably because it gives them a safe experience of a potentially catastrophic scenario.\nThey find it pleasurable, and pleasure is evolution's way of motivating us toward adaptive behaviour.\nHorror is pleasurable to many people because it lets us play with negative emotions and develop coping strategies. We learn what it feels like to be truly afraid, and we learn how to handle negative emotion.\nHow, then, does horror work? My research suggests that horror works by exploiting an ancient set of biological defence mechanisms—an evolved fear system, which we share with other animals. But humans are uniquely imaginative, and we use our evolved imagination to travel into virtual worlds that are full of danger.\nThere are good reasons for watching a horror film, even if you're not a loyal horror fan. If you make it through the film in one piece, you'll probably experience a strong sense of mastery, a sense that you were able to make it through an appalling experience. Anyway, watching a horror film makes you better at handling your own fear, and who knows when that will become critically relevant?", "51": "An awakening has been taking place in the physical world against the beauty model that has been dictated to us for years. But in the digital arena, social media determines what is considered beautiful.\nThe two opposing struggles are taking place in parallel. In the physical world, the struggle goes against the underlying pressure exerted on women to conform to an unrealistic beauty ideal. As part of the struggle, various media outlets have presented women whose bodies don't correspond to the so-called ideal. All those women who had previously been excluded from the covers of magazines, television series and the public agenda, have become \"legitimate\". At the same time, a group of influencers have begun to upload to social media photos of themselves without makeup, and even photos in which they highlight supposed flaws.\nTechnology has reshaped our beauty ideal and is doing a great job communicating that message to the masses. One of the bizarre legacies of the past decade is the popularity of the \"cyborg look\", which illustrates what Americans will look like in 2050.\nThe cyborg look spread rapidly. Today, however, the Instagram face has become the new beauty ideal.\nThe internalization of accepted beauty norms is much more effective when there is active involvement in the learning process. The active involvement of users is reflected in the gamified(游戏化的) interaction offered by the social media platforms—the ability to like, write a comment, compare, share.\nOnce the desired beauty ideal has been internalized, users are given tools or features to change their appearance to suit the accepted beauty ideal such as editing the image, choosing the ideal filter, the right background.\nA survey conducted in the United States revealed that more than 50% filter the images before posting them. And you will not be surprised to hear that the majority of them are women. One of the significant consequences of obsessive filtering is the emerging tendency to treat oneself as an object to be observed and valued, in the same way another person observes and judges from the side.\nThe effect of the filters is already far beyond amiable amusement. The filters and the entire game played on the networks affect the mental health of the users. According to a study, apps like Instagram, Snapchat and FaceTune allow users to achieve a level of perfection that was previously only observed in beauty magazines.\nEven though humanity has always cherished beauty, in the last decade our obsession with looks has reached an unprecedented peak. The time spent on social media creates an urge to achieve an impossible beauty ideal so powerful that the only thing that can fix it is not cosmetic intervention, but mental health care.", "52": "An awakening has been taking place in the physical world against the beauty model that has been dictated to us for years. But in the digital arena, social media determines what is considered beautiful.\nThe two opposing struggles are taking place in parallel. In the physical world, the struggle goes against the underlying pressure exerted on women to conform to an unrealistic beauty ideal. As part of the struggle, various media outlets have presented women whose bodies don't correspond to the so-called ideal. All those women who had previously been excluded from the covers of magazines, television series and the public agenda, have become \"legitimate\". At the same time, a group of influencers have begun to upload to social media photos of themselves without makeup, and even photos in which they highlight supposed flaws.\nTechnology has reshaped our beauty ideal and is doing a great job communicating that message to the masses. One of the bizarre legacies of the past decade is the popularity of the \"cyborg look\", which illustrates what Americans will look like in 2050.\nThe cyborg look spread rapidly. Today, however, the Instagram face has become the new beauty ideal.\nThe internalization of accepted beauty norms is much more effective when there is active involvement in the learning process. The active involvement of users is reflected in the gamified(游戏化的) interaction offered by the social media platforms—the ability to like, write a comment, compare, share.\nOnce the desired beauty ideal has been internalized, users are given tools or features to change their appearance to suit the accepted beauty ideal such as editing the image, choosing the ideal filter, the right background.\nA survey conducted in the United States revealed that more than 50% filter the images before posting them. And you will not be surprised to hear that the majority of them are women. One of the significant consequences of obsessive filtering is the emerging tendency to treat oneself as an object to be observed and valued, in the same way another person observes and judges from the side.\nThe effect of the filters is already far beyond amiable amusement. The filters and the entire game played on the networks affect the mental health of the users. According to a study, apps like Instagram, Snapchat and FaceTune allow users to achieve a level of perfection that was previously only observed in beauty magazines.\nEven though humanity has always cherished beauty, in the last decade our obsession with looks has reached an unprecedented peak. The time spent on social media creates an urge to achieve an impossible beauty ideal so powerful that the only thing that can fix it is not cosmetic intervention, but mental health care.", "53": "An awakening has been taking place in the physical world against the beauty model that has been dictated to us for years. But in the digital arena, social media determines what is considered beautiful.\nThe two opposing struggles are taking place in parallel. In the physical world, the struggle goes against the underlying pressure exerted on women to conform to an unrealistic beauty ideal. As part of the struggle, various media outlets have presented women whose bodies don't correspond to the so-called ideal. All those women who had previously been excluded from the covers of magazines, television series and the public agenda, have become \"legitimate\". At the same time, a group of influencers have begun to upload to social media photos of themselves without makeup, and even photos in which they highlight supposed flaws.\nTechnology has reshaped our beauty ideal and is doing a great job communicating that message to the masses. One of the bizarre legacies of the past decade is the popularity of the \"cyborg look\", which illustrates what Americans will look like in 2050.\nThe cyborg look spread rapidly. Today, however, the Instagram face has become the new beauty ideal.\nThe internalization of accepted beauty norms is much more effective when there is active involvement in the learning process. The active involvement of users is reflected in the gamified(游戏化的) interaction offered by the social media platforms—the ability to like, write a comment, compare, share.\nOnce the desired beauty ideal has been internalized, users are given tools or features to change their appearance to suit the accepted beauty ideal such as editing the image, choosing the ideal filter, the right background.\nA survey conducted in the United States revealed that more than 50% filter the images before posting them. And you will not be surprised to hear that the majority of them are women. One of the significant consequences of obsessive filtering is the emerging tendency to treat oneself as an object to be observed and valued, in the same way another person observes and judges from the side.\nThe effect of the filters is already far beyond amiable amusement. The filters and the entire game played on the networks affect the mental health of the users. According to a study, apps like Instagram, Snapchat and FaceTune allow users to achieve a level of perfection that was previously only observed in beauty magazines.\nEven though humanity has always cherished beauty, in the last decade our obsession with looks has reached an unprecedented peak. The time spent on social media creates an urge to achieve an impossible beauty ideal so powerful that the only thing that can fix it is not cosmetic intervention, but mental health care.", "54": "An awakening has been taking place in the physical world against the beauty model that has been dictated to us for years. But in the digital arena, social media determines what is considered beautiful.\nThe two opposing struggles are taking place in parallel. In the physical world, the struggle goes against the underlying pressure exerted on women to conform to an unrealistic beauty ideal. As part of the struggle, various media outlets have presented women whose bodies don't correspond to the so-called ideal. All those women who had previously been excluded from the covers of magazines, television series and the public agenda, have become \"legitimate\". At the same time, a group of influencers have begun to upload to social media photos of themselves without makeup, and even photos in which they highlight supposed flaws.\nTechnology has reshaped our beauty ideal and is doing a great job communicating that message to the masses. One of the bizarre legacies of the past decade is the popularity of the \"cyborg look\", which illustrates what Americans will look like in 2050.\nThe cyborg look spread rapidly. Today, however, the Instagram face has become the new beauty ideal.\nThe internalization of accepted beauty norms is much more effective when there is active involvement in the learning process. The active involvement of users is reflected in the gamified(游戏化的) interaction offered by the social media platforms—the ability to like, write a comment, compare, share.\nOnce the desired beauty ideal has been internalized, users are given tools or features to change their appearance to suit the accepted beauty ideal such as editing the image, choosing the ideal filter, the right background.\nA survey conducted in the United States revealed that more than 50% filter the images before posting them. And you will not be surprised to hear that the majority of them are women. One of the significant consequences of obsessive filtering is the emerging tendency to treat oneself as an object to be observed and valued, in the same way another person observes and judges from the side.\nThe effect of the filters is already far beyond amiable amusement. The filters and the entire game played on the networks affect the mental health of the users. According to a study, apps like Instagram, Snapchat and FaceTune allow users to achieve a level of perfection that was previously only observed in beauty magazines.\nEven though humanity has always cherished beauty, in the last decade our obsession with looks has reached an unprecedented peak. The time spent on social media creates an urge to achieve an impossible beauty ideal so powerful that the only thing that can fix it is not cosmetic intervention, but mental health care.", "55": "An awakening has been taking place in the physical world against the beauty model that has been dictated to us for years. But in the digital arena, social media determines what is considered beautiful.\nThe two opposing struggles are taking place in parallel. In the physical world, the struggle goes against the underlying pressure exerted on women to conform to an unrealistic beauty ideal. As part of the struggle, various media outlets have presented women whose bodies don't correspond to the so-called ideal. All those women who had previously been excluded from the covers of magazines, television series and the public agenda, have become \"legitimate\". At the same time, a group of influencers have begun to upload to social media photos of themselves without makeup, and even photos in which they highlight supposed flaws.\nTechnology has reshaped our beauty ideal and is doing a great job communicating that message to the masses. One of the bizarre legacies of the past decade is the popularity of the \"cyborg look\", which illustrates what Americans will look like in 2050.\nThe cyborg look spread rapidly. Today, however, the Instagram face has become the new beauty ideal.\nThe internalization of accepted beauty norms is much more effective when there is active involvement in the learning process. The active involvement of users is reflected in the gamified(游戏化的) interaction offered by the social media platforms—the ability to like, write a comment, compare, share.\nOnce the desired beauty ideal has been internalized, users are given tools or features to change their appearance to suit the accepted beauty ideal such as editing the image, choosing the ideal filter, the right background.\nA survey conducted in the United States revealed that more than 50% filter the images before posting them. And you will not be surprised to hear that the majority of them are women. One of the significant consequences of obsessive filtering is the emerging tendency to treat oneself as an object to be observed and valued, in the same way another person observes and judges from the side.\nThe effect of the filters is already far beyond amiable amusement. The filters and the entire game played on the networks affect the mental health of the users. According to a study, apps like Instagram, Snapchat and FaceTune allow users to achieve a level of perfection that was previously only observed in beauty magazines.\nEven though humanity has always cherished beauty, in the last decade our obsession with looks has reached an unprecedented peak. The time spent on social media creates an urge to achieve an impossible beauty ideal so powerful that the only thing that can fix it is not cosmetic intervention, but mental health care."}, "questions": [{"id": "2024-12-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What are the speakers most probably doing?", "options": ["A) Preparing for a job interview.", "B) Writing a work report together.", "C) Going through a couple of issues the company faces.", "D) Discussing the woman's annual performance review."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题） Discussing the woman's annual performance review.\n【定位】OK then, this here is your yearly performance review. You've been with the company for 3\nyears and under my direct supervision for the past 12 months.\n那好，这是你的年度绩效评估。你在公司已经三年，过去十二个月在我的直接管理之下。\n【信号】开头场景定调句。对话第一句 this here is your yearly performance review 就开门见山点\n出场合，是典型的“开头定调”信号：主旨情境题的答案往往就埋在第一句对场合的交代里。\n【替换】this here is your yearly performance review ↔\nDiscussing the woman's annual performance review\nyearly等于annual\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音是上司给在职三年的下属做评估，全程没有应聘、招人的内容，Preparing for a job\ninterview. 凭“职场”联想硬套，属未提及。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音里 report 是指已经写好的评估报告(Your report is mostly good)，男方说 the report\nis yours to take，并非两人“一起写报告”。Writing a work report together. 盗用了原词\nreport 却把“一起过报告”偷换成“一起写报告”。\n借⽤原⽂：Your report is mostly good\nC) 原词陷阱\n男方确实说了 there are a couple of issues we must discuss，但那只是这次绩效面谈的\n一部分(后半段才谈问题)，并非两人整体“只在过几个公司问题”。Going through a couple\nof issues the company faces. 盗用 a couple of issues 把局部当成了全局，且 issues 是\n关于“她”的，不是“公司面临的问题”。\n借⽤原⽂：there are a couple of issues we must discuss", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the woman say she appreciates?", "options": ["A) The overall culture of the company.", "B) The instruction from her supervisor.", "C) The honesty of the manager.", "D) The recognition of her merits."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） The recognition of her merits.\n【定位】Thank you. I appreciate the recognition.\n谢谢，我很感激这份认可。\n【信号】被问者的直接应答。男方刚夸完她勤奋、有分析力、令公司满意，女方紧接着应答 I\nappreciate the recognition，这句“道谢 + appreciate”正是与题干 appreciate 直接对应\n的应答句，信号极明确。\n【替换】I appreciate the recognition ↔ The recognition of her merits.\n认可即对优点的认可\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThe overall culture of the company. 录音从未谈“公司文化”，只是借 company 一词(男方\n多次提 the company)制造“职场”联想，属未提及。\nB) 原词陷阱\nThe instruction from her supervisor. 借男方自称 supervisor(under my direct\nsupervision)制造干扰，但女方感激的是“认可”而非“上级的指示”，盗用 supervision 一词\n答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：under my direct supervision\nC) 偷换概念\nThe honesty of the manager. 中“经理诚实”录音并未作为她感激的内容提及；女方后文虽\n说 to be perfectly honest，但那是她自述坦率，与“感激经理诚实”无关，属偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：to be perfectly honest", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What has given rise to some complaints about the woman?", "options": ["A) Her inadequate language proficiency.", "B) Her inability to interact with colleagues properly.", "C) Her inappropriate behavior at company meetings.", "D) Her simplistic approach to dealing with others."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Her inability to interact with colleagues properly.\n【定位】This leads me to the not-so-good part. This mostly centers around one behavioral aspect—\nyour ability to work with others.\n这就引到不太好的部分了。它主要集中在一个行为方面——你与他人合作的能力。\n【信号】转折后才是落点。男方用 the not-so-good part 把话题从表扬转向批评，这是明确的转向\n信号；转折之后点出的 your ability to work with others 才是抱怨的真正所指，前半的表\n扬都是铺垫。\n【替换】your ability to work with others ↔ Her inability to interact with colleagues properly.\n合作能力即与同事互动\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nHer inadequate language proficiency. 录音从未说她“语言能力不足”；抱怨针对的是\ncommunication style(沟通方式)，与“语言水平”是两回事，属偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：complaints raised about your communication style\nC) 原词陷阱\nHer inappropriate behavior at company meetings. 盗用了 meetings(女方说 we\nalready had like three meetings about this)，但 meetings 是“就此事开过的三次会”，不\n是“她开会时行为不当”。抱怨的内容是沟通方式，与开会表现无关。\n借⽤原⽂：we already had like three meetings about this\nD) 张冠李戴\nHer simplistic approach to dealing with others. 借第6段女方自评报告 it's a little\nsimplistic perhaps 里的 simplistic 一词制造干扰，但那句是她评价“这份评估”有点简单，\n与“她待人方式简单”无关，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：it's a little simplistic perhaps", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "Why doesn't the woman like to write long emails?", "options": ["A) To avoid offending the recipients.", "B) To show her unique writing style.", "C) To save time.", "D) To be frank."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） To save time.\n【定位】I don't like to write long emails, because, frankly, I think they're a waste of time. Conciseness\nis efficiency.\n我不喜欢写长邮件，因为说实话，我觉得那是浪费时间。简洁就是效率。\n【信号】因果because直给原因。她说 I don't like to write long emails, because… I think they're\na waste of time，because 之后就是她亲口给出的理由——“浪费时间”，因果信号直白，\n是最干净的答案句。\n【替换】I think they're a waste of time ↔ To save time.\n不浪费时间即省时间\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nTo avoid offending the recipients. 盗用她说的 it was never my intention to offend\nanyone，但“无意冒犯”是她对抱怨的辩解，不是她“不写长邮件”的原因。她不写长邮件的理\n由明确是“浪费时间”，A 答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：it was never my intention to offend anyone\nB) 未提及\nTo show her unique writing style. 录音从未说她不写长邮件是为“展示独特文风”，纯属凭\n“写邮件”话题臆造，属未提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\nTo be frank. 盗用她话里的 frankly(frankly, I think they're a waste of time) 与 to be\nperfectly honest，但 frankly 只是她说话的口吻插入语，不是“不写长邮件”的目的。真正原\n因在 frankly 之后的 a waste of time，D 把语气词错当成了动机。\n借⽤原⽂：frankly, I think they're a waste of time", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the woman say she has always wanted to do since childhood?", "options": ["A) Provide medical service to the community.", "B) Make healthcare in her hometown the best.", "C) Obtain a doctoral degree in internal medicine.", "D) Have a profound impact on people around her."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Provide medical service to the community.\n【定位】As a child, I remember thinking how great it would be to serve the community and\ncure people!\n小时候，我就记得自己在想：能服务社区、治病救人该有多好啊！\n【信号】As a child 提示童年愿望。信号是时间状语 As a child——它直接对应题干的 since\nchildhood，把\"从小的愿望\"标了出来；紧跟其后的 to serve the community and cure\npeople 就是她从小想做的事。\n【替换】serve the community ↔ Provide medical service to the community\nserve=provide service\ncure people ↔ medical service\ncure people 概括为 medical service\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 用了录音里 I come from a small town where healthcare was never the best 中的\nhealthcare 与\"家乡\"概念，但录音说的是\"家乡医疗从来不算好\"，她的愿望是去服务，并没\n有说要\"把家乡医疗变成最好\"。这是把原词拼成一个原文没说的目标。\n借⽤原⽂：I come from a small town where healthcare was never the best\nC) 未提及\nC 说\"拿内科博士学位\"，但录音里她只说想当医生、想专攻内科，从没提过\"博士学位\"。这\n是把后文的 internal medicine 嫁接到一个没出现过的\"读博\"目标上。\nD) 与原文相反\nD\"对身边人产生深远影响\"借了录音 that had a profound effect on me 的 profound\neffect，但原文是\"家乡医疗状况对她产生了深远影响\"——是别的事影响\"她\"，不是她要去\n影响别人。方向被调了个个儿。\n借⽤原⽂：that had a profound effect on me", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What do we learn about the woman's parents from the conversation?", "options": ["A) They have constantly urged her to study hard.", "B) They have worked hard to finance her education.", "C) They have tried to create a positive learning environment.", "D) They have pursued the family's dreams together with her."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They have constantly urged her to study hard.\n【定位】I would certainly say my parents have been a key factor. They always pushed me to\nstudy hard.\n我肯定会说，我父母是关键因素。他们总是督促我努力学习。\n【信号】被问者应答里的 my parents。信号是被问者直接点名 my parents have been a key\nfactor——对话题答案落在回答方，她一提到 parents，下一句 They always pushed me\nto study hard 就是关于父母的核心信息。\n【替换】always pushed me to study hard ↔ constantly urged her to study hard\npushed=urged，always=constantly\n【排除】B) 偷换概念\nB\"努力挣钱供她读书\"利用了录音 I certainly wouldn't be here without their support 里\n的 support，但 support 只是泛指\"支持\"，录音从未说是\"经济上出钱（finance）\"。这是\n把一个泛词坐实成具体的金钱资助。\n借⽤原⽂：I certainly wouldn't be here without their support\nC) 张冠李戴\nC\"营造积极学习环境\"盗用了录音 a positive environment conducive to my working\nhard，但说这环境的是\"老师、朋友等她周围的人\"带来的，原文并没把\"营造环境\"安在父母\n头上。这是张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：surrounded by a positive environment conducive to my working hard\nD) 未提及\nD 说父母\"和她一起追求家庭梦想\"，录音只说追梦是\"她自己的\"（pursuing my\ndreams），并无父母与她\"共同追梦\"之说。属凭空添加。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "Why does the woman say she wants to choose internal medicine as her specialty?", "options": ["A) It is a key medical branch conducive to realizing her dreams.", "B) It connects many other specialties with its broad coverage.", "C) It has a long history in the man's prestigious institution.", "D) It is a medical branch both of her parents specialize in."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It connects many other specialties with its broad coverage.\n【定位】But I find it the most intriguing, because it covers so much and ties together so many\nother specialties.\n但我觉得它最有意思，因为它覆盖面很广，又把许多其他专科串联在一起。\n【信号】because 领出真正理由。信号是因果连词 because——它后面 it covers so much and\nties together so many other specialties 才是她选内科的真正原因；before because 的\nmost intriguing 只是感受，理由在 because 之后。\n【替换】it covers so much ↔ its broad coverage\ncovers so much=broad coverage\nties together so many other specialties ↔ connects many other specialties\nties together=connects\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA\"有助于实现她的梦想\"里的 dreams 确在录音别处出现过（pursuing my dreams），但\n她解释\"为何选内科\"时给的理由是\"覆盖广、连专科\"，从未说内科\"有助于实现梦想\"。这是\n把别处的词搬来当本题理由。\n借⽤原⽂：pursuing my dreams\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 说内科\"在这所名校历史悠久\"，录音里 prestigious institution（名校）确出现过，但谈\n的是\"学校的声望与竞争\"，根本没说内科\"历史悠久\"。把名校话题硬接到\"内科有历史\"上。\n借⽤原⽂：prestigious institution\nD) 未提及\nD\"父母都专攻这一科\"，录音里父母只\"督促她学习\"，从没说过父母是医生、更没说专攻内\n科。这是把 parents 与 internal medicine 两个真词强行拼成一个假关系。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "How does the man describe the woman's school grades?", "options": ["A) Problematic.", "B) Competitive.", "C) Inconsistent.", "D) Trustworthy."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （态度观点题） Inconsistent.\n【定位】You do get top grades here and there, but you are not very… Shall we say \"consistent\"?\n你确实这儿那儿拿过一些顶尖成绩，但你不太……怎么说呢，\"稳定\"？\n【信号】转折 but 后才是真评价。信号是转折词 but——but 之前的 You do get top grades 是先\n扬，but 之后 you are not very… consistent 才是男方真正的评价：成绩不够稳定。态度题\n的落点在转折后。\n【替换】you are not very… Shall we say \"consistent\"? ↔ Inconsistent\nnot consistent=inconsistent\na bit of a mixed bag ↔ Inconsistent\nmixed bag=时好时坏=inconsistent\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA\"有问题的\"借了录音 I see no problem in making you a conditional offer 里的\nproblem，但那句说的是\"给你有条件录取没问题\"，是对\"录取\"说的，不是评价成绩\"有问\n题\"。盗了 problem 却换了对象。\n借⽤原⽂：I see no problem in making you a conditional offer\nB) 张冠李戴\nB\"有竞争力的\"盗用录音 a highly competitive and promising student body 的\ncompetitive，但那是男方夸\"本校生源竞争激烈\"，与\"她的成绩\"无关。张冠李戴到了成绩\n头上。\n借⽤原⽂：a highly competitive and promising student body\nD) 与原文相反\nD\"可信赖的\"是褒义，但男方对成绩的整体态度是负面的（mixed bag、not consistent、\nwarned），方向完全相反。把先扬后抑听成了纯表扬。\n借⽤原⽂：consider yourself warned", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "Why do we often have to choose photos that include a street sign to log into our email accounts?", "options": ["A) To prevent hackers from cracking our secret codes.", "B) To show that all the guidelines are being followed.", "C) To provide evidence that passwords are forgotten.", "D) To convince our computer that we are human."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） To convince our computer that we are human.\n【定位】More often than not, you then have to choose photos that include a street sign, as this\napparently provides solid evidence to your computer that you are, in fact, a human being.\n你往往还得去选那些含有街道标志的照片，因为这显然能向你的电脑提供确凿的证据，证明\n你其实是一个人类。\n【信号】原因信号 as 引出真意。答案就在 as this apparently provides solid evidence to your\ncomputer that you are, in fact, a human being 里的 as 之后——as 在这里是『因为』，\n专门交代前面『选含街道标志照片』这个动作的目的，是典型的原因信号。\n【替换】provides solid evidence to your computer that you are, in fact, a human being ↔\nTo convince our computer that we are human\n提供证据=使信服，同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项里的 hackers 让人联想原文开头 hack into the Pentagon，但那只是开篇打比方说登\n录难，与『选街道标志照片是为了什么』无关，属听到 hack 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：trying to hack into the Pentagon\nB) 原词陷阱\nall the guidelines are being followed 借用原文 If you follow all of the guidelines，但\n原文那句是在罗列守规则有多麻烦，并非解释选照片的目的，词对了、对应错了。\n借⽤原⽂：If you follow all of the guidelines\nC) 未提及\npasswords are forgotten 把后文『人会忘密码』这个话题硬塞进本题，但选照片是为了证\n明你是人，跟『提供忘记密码的证据』毫无关系，原文也没这么说。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What do researchers at a university in Finland aim to do in their new study?", "options": ["A) Put an end to the hacking phenomenon.", "B) Help people remember their passwords.", "C) Reduce the intricacy of the password itself.", "D) Explain the need for different email accounts."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （目的题） Help people remember their passwords.\n【定位】Now a new peer-reviewed study by researchers at Aalto University in Finland is\nattempting to figure out why we forget our passwords in order to help put an end to this\nfrustrating phenomenon.\n如今，芬兰阿尔托大学研究者开展的一项新的同行评审研究，正试图弄清我们为什么会忘记\n密码，以期帮助终结这一令人沮丧的现象。\n【信号】目的信号 in order to help。研究者的真正目标由 in order to help put an end to this\nfrustrating phenomenon 中的 in order to 引出——in order to 是直白的目的信号，它后\n面才是研究的最终意图。\n【替换】help put an end to this frustrating phenomenon ↔ Help people remember their passwords\n代词 this 指回忘密码，概括等值\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nPut an end to the hacking phenomenon 直接搬原文 put an end to this frustrating\nphenomenon 的 put an end to，却把对象偷换成 hacking（黑客）；原文要终结的是\n『忘密码』这一令人沮丧的现象，不是黑客现象。\n借⽤原⽂：in order to help put an end to this frustrating phenomenon\nC) 原词陷阱\nReduce the intricacy of the password itself 借了原文反复出现的 the password itself，\n但研究的发现是『记不记得住与密码本身关系不大』，从未说要降低密码复杂度，是把原词\n拼成原文没有的目标。\n借⽤原⽂：less to do with the password itself\nD) 原词陷阱\nExplain the need for different email accounts 让人想到原文 a different password for\nevery one of your accounts，但那是开篇讲守规则的麻烦，与芬兰研究的目标无关，\naccounts 这个词被挪用来骗选。\n借⽤原⽂：a different password for every one of your accounts", "points": ["听力·篇章", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What do the researchers advise websites to do at the end of the passage?", "options": ["A) Innovate technologies to ensure the safety of users' accounts.", "B) Provide incentives for the application of creative passwords.", "C) Explore the possibility of using simpler secret codes.", "D) Take steps to encourage users to log in more often."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Take steps to encourage users to log in more often.\n【定位】The researchers advise websites to include more of an incentive for users to log in more often\nin order to help them remember their secret codes.\n研究者建议网站多设置一些激励，促使用户更频繁地登录，以帮助他们记住自己的密码。\n【信号】建议信号 advise 直接给出对策。答案由 The researchers advise websites to include\nmore of an incentive for users to log in more often 里的 advise 直接引出——advise\n是建议信号，它后面 websites to... 的内容就是研究者给出的对策。\n【替换】include more of an incentive for users to log in more often ↔\nTake steps to encourage users to log in more often\n设激励=采取措施鼓励，同义改写\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nInnovate technologies to ensure the safety of users' accounts 蹭全篇的安全 /\naccounts 话题，但研究者的建议是加登录激励，从未让网站去『创新技术保障账户安\n全』，是凭话题耳熟编出的拔高项。\nB) 原词陷阱\nProvide incentives for the application of creative passwords 直接搬原文 include more\nof an incentive 的 incentive，最易骗『听到 incentive 就选』，但原文是为『更常登录』\n设激励，不是为『使用创意密码』，对象被偷换了。\n借⽤原⽂：include more of an incentive for users to log in more often\nC) 原词陷阱\nExplore the possibility of using simpler secret codes 借了原文结尾的 secret codes，但\n原文那句是说激励的目的（帮人记住密码），研究者并没建议用更简单的密码，词对、意思\n不对。\n借⽤原⽂：help them remember their secret codes", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What was the problem Jenny Weiner encountered four years ago?", "options": ["A) Her work no longer interested her.", "B) Her training app did not fit her.", "C) Her fitness fell into a slump.", "D) Her business deteriorated."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Her fitness fell into a slump.\n【定位】Four years ago, Jenny Weiner was in a fitness slump.\n四年前，珍妮·韦纳的健身状态陷入了低谷。\n【信号】开篇点题句 + 与题干 four years ago 时间对齐。录音开头 Four years ago, Jenny\nWeiner was in a fitness slump. 的时间状语 four years ago 与题干完全对齐，是篇章开篇\n交代背景的点题句，承载本题答案。\n【替换】in a fitness slump ↔ Her fitness fell into a slump.\n同词换句式：介词短语改写成主谓句，核心词 fitness slump 不变\n【排除】A) 未提及\nHer work no longer interested her. 说的是 \"工作不再有趣\"，但录音通篇只谈健身、训练\napp，从未提及她的 work 或兴趣转变，属凭常识脑补的未提及项。\nB) 原词陷阱\nHer training app did not fit her. 借用了原文反复出现的 training 与 app，可录音里 She\nthen discovered the fitness phone app Slimmeretics. 表明 app 是她发现后用来解决问\n题的工具，并非四年前遇到的问题本身，听到 app 就选正中圈套。\n借⽤原⽂：She then discovered the fitness phone app Slimmeretics.\nD) 偷换概念\nHer business deteriorated. 把 \"健身低谷\" 偷换成 \"生意变差\"，business 与原文 fitness\nslump 词形相近、都是 \"某事变糟\" 的格局，但录音谈的是身体健身状态而非商业，方向被\n悄悄换掉。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What can the fitness phone app Slimmeretics do according to the passage?", "options": ["A) Help users keep track of their fitness levels.", "B) Design personalised training programmes.", "C) Strengthen ties among users worldwide.", "D) Select well-qualified human coaches."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Design personalised training programmes.\n【定位】A virtual coach then uses that information to suggest a personalised training regime.\n随后一个虚拟教练会利用这些信息，推荐一套个性化的训练方案。\n【信号】功能描述句：suggest 引出 app 提供的核心服务。A virtual coach then uses that\ninformation to suggest a personalised training regime. 用 suggest（推荐/给出）这一\n动作直接交代了 app 的核心功能——量身定制训练方案，是承载本题答案的功能描述句。\n【替换】suggest a personalised training regime ↔ Design personalised training programmes.\n近义词替换：suggest→Design、regime→programmes，personalised 原样保留\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nHelp users keep track of their fitness levels. 看似贴合 app 的智能形象，且原文确有\ngives feedback 与 adjust future training sessions 这类近似 \"追踪/调整\" 的表述，但原文\n说的是据反馈调整训练，从未说帮用户 \"记录健身水平\"，是借相近语境编出的功能。\n借⽤原⽂：the app's artificial intelligence software system gives feedback\nC) 原词陷阱\nStrengthen ties among users worldwide. 借用了原文 its other 53 million users around\nthe world 里 \"全球用户\" 的字眼，可原文只是说 app 收集这些用户的反馈来优化训练，根\n本没提让用户彼此 \"加强联系\"，听到 worldwide users 就选会掉坑。\n借⽤原⽂：its other 53 million users around the world\nD) 原词陷阱\nSelect well-qualified human coaches. 借用原文反复出现的 human personal\ntrainers/coach，可原文明说 app 配的是 A virtual coach（虚拟教练），并非 app 去挑选\n真人教练；末尾还有专家称 apps cannot replace human personal trainers，方向恰相\n反。\n借⽤原⽂：A virtual coach then uses that information", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "Why does the passage say Jenny Weiner now finds the workouts exciting?", "options": ["A) They are never repeated.", "B) They help enrich her life.", "C) They are of no extreme intensity.", "D) They keep her focused on her goal."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They are never repeated.\n【定位】Now Ms. Weiner finds the workouts exciting. This is because no two are ever the same.\n如今韦纳女士觉得训练很有意思。这是因为没有任何两次训练是相同的。\n【信号】因果信号 This is because 紧跟其后的句子即原因。原文先说她觉得训练有趣，紧接 This is\nbecause no two are ever the same. 用因果信号 This is because 引出原因——没有两次\n训练是一样的，正是本题答案落点。\n【替换】no two are ever the same ↔ They are never repeated.\n肯否结构转换：\"没有两次相同\" = \"从不重复\"，意思等值\n【排除】B) 答非所问\nThey help enrich her life. 听来正面、也像 app 的好处，但题问的是 \"为何觉得训练\nexciting\"，原文给的原因明确是 no two are ever the same，而非 \"丰富生活\"；该项答的\n是别的问题。\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey are of no extreme intensity. 借用了原文 her workouts lacked intensity 中的\nintensity，可那句讲的是四年前训练缺乏强度的旧问题，与 \"现在为何觉得有趣\" 无关，听\n到 intensity 就选即中招。\n借⽤原⽂：her workouts lacked intensity\nD) 原词陷阱\nThey keep her focused on her goal. 借用原文 your desired goals 里的 goal，可原文谈\ngoal 只是说输入资料时填写目标，并未把 \"专注目标\" 说成她觉得训练有趣的原因，原因仍\n是 no two are ever the same。\n借⽤原⽂：your desired goals", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What do some experts think of fitness apps like Slimmeretics?", "options": ["A) They will not be able to take the place of human personal trainers.", "B) They will not be able to comprehend some of the profiles users put in.", "C) They cannot lead to optimal results with their mathematical approach.", "D) They cannot match humans in arranging meticulous workout schedules."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） They will not be able to take the place of human personal trainers.\n【定位】However, some experts think that apps cannot replace human personal trainers.\n然而，一些专家认为这些 app 无法取代真人私人教练。\n【信号】转折 However + 评价主体 some experts 直接表态。原文前面一直夸 app 效果好，\nHowever, some experts think that apps cannot replace human personal trainers. 用\n转折 However 转向、并由评价主体 some experts 直陈观点，转折后才是本题真意。\n【替换】apps cannot replace human personal trainers ↔\nThey will not be able to take the place of human personal trainers.\n近义短语替换：replace = take the place of，human personal trainers 原样保留\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nThey will not be able to comprehend some of the profiles users put in. 借用了原文 It\nwill not comprehend that there are competing interests 的 comprehend，以及输入环\n节的 profile，可原文说 app 不能理解的是 \"人类行为/相互竞争的利益\"，而非看不懂用户\n填的 profiles，对象被偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：It will not comprehend that there are competing interests outside of health and fitness.\nC) 与原文相反\nThey cannot lead to optimal results with their mathematical approach. 盗用了原文\nmathematical approach 与 optimal results，可原文是正面说 The app's mathematical\napproach can arrange a more meticulous workout schedule. This has led to optimal\nresults.——数学方法\"能\"带来最优结果，选项加了 cannot 把极性反了。\n借⽤原⽂：The app's mathematical approach can arrange a more meticulous workout schedule.\nThis has led to optimal results.\nD) 与原文相反\nThey cannot match humans in arranging meticulous workout schedules. 借用原文\nmeticulous workout schedule，可原文是正面称 The app's mathematical approach\ncan arrange a more meticulous workout schedule.——app 能编排更精细的计划，并非\n说它在这点上不如真人，方向相反。\n借⽤原⽂：The app's mathematical approach can arrange a more meticulous workout schedule.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What do we learn from the conclusion of the Oxford University researchers?", "options": ["A) All societies are built upon the cornerstones of basic values.", "B) Everyone everywhere demonstrates seven character traits.", "C) All societies are kept together by seven basic moral rules.", "D) Everyone everywhere shares a universal moral standard."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） All societies are kept together by seven basic moral rules.\n【定位】All societies are held together by seven universal moral rules, which include deferring to\nsuperiors and respecting the property of others, Oxford University has concluded.\n牛津大学得出结论：所有社会都靠七条普世道德规则维系在一起，其中包括服从尊长、尊重\n他人财物。\n【信号】结论标志词 concluded。句末的 Oxford University has concluded 是明确的结论信号，\n加之这句出现在篇章开头点题处，正是篇章独白把核心论点摆在开头的典型位置，听到\nconcluded 就该锁定这句是答案句。\n【替换】All societies are held together by seven universal moral rules ↔\nAll societies are kept together by seven basic moral rules\nheld together 同义改写为 kept together\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nAll societies are built upon the cornerstones of basic values. 把第一段后文的 basic\nvalues of charity and brotherhood are the cornerstones of successful societies 的原\n词 cornerstones、basic values 搬了过来，但那句讲的是成功社会的基石，并非研究的总\n结论本身，听到熟词就选会落进这个陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：basic values of charity and brotherhood are the cornerstones of successful societies\nB) 偷换概念\nEveryone everywhere demonstrates seven character traits. 借了原文出现过的 seven\n和第二段的 The character traits held for every kind of community，但研究的结论说的\n是七条道德规则（rules），而非七种性格特质，把 rules 偷换成 character traits 就跑偏\n了。\n借⽤原⽂：The character traits held for every kind of community\nD) 偷换概念\nEveryone everywhere shares a universal moral standard. 用了第二段 Everyone\neverywhere shares a common moral code 的熟悉句式，但原文说的是共享一套七条规则\n的道德准则（code），D 把它笼统说成 a universal moral standard，丢掉了结论里七条\n规则这一核心，不如 C 精确对应题干问的结论。\n借⽤原⽂：Everyone everywhere shares a common moral code", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What did the new study by Oxford researchers aim to do?", "options": ["A) Ascertain whether deferring to authority was confined to right-wing people.", "B) Find out whether different societies had different versions of morality.", "C) Make clear whether all societies faced the same moral issues.", "D) Find out whether left-wing people still had a group loyalty."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （目的题） Find out whether different societies had different versions of morality.\n【定位】The study published in Current Anthropology is the largest and most comprehensive and\nwidespread survey of morals ever conducted and aimed to find out whether different societies\nhad different versions of morality.\n这项发表于《当代人类学》的研究是有史以来规模最大、最全面、覆盖最广的道德调查，旨\n在弄清不同社会是否有不同版本的道德观。\n【信号】意图标志 aimed to find out。句中的 aimed to find out whether different societies\nhad different versions of morality 用 aimed to 直陈研究目的，这是目的题最直接的信\n号，听到 aimed to 即可锁定。\n【替换】aimed to find out whether different societies had different versions of morality ↔\nFind out whether different societies had different versions of morality\n近乎原句，仅去掉 aimed to\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nAscertain whether deferring to authority was confined to right-wing people. 把第二\n段 Dr. Curry 那句里的 deferring to authority 和 right-wing 原词搬来，但那是他举例说明\n规则的普世性，并非这项研究本身要弄清的目标，听到熟词就选会被带偏。\n借⽤原⽂：deferring to authority\nC) 偷换概念\nMake clear whether all societies faced the same moral issues. 听上去和研究目的沾\n边，但原文目的是查不同社会是否有不同版本的道德，C 反向说成所有社会是否面对相同道\n德问题，方向被改了。\n借⽤原⽂：whether different societies had different versions of morality\nD) 原词陷阱\nFind out whether left-wing people still had a group loyalty. 借了第二段 left-wing\npeople will still have a group loyalty 的原话，但那是 Dr. Curry 论证规则普世性的例子，\n不是研究要查明的目标，属典型的盗用原句却答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：left-wing people will still have a group loyalty", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What does Harvey Whitehouse think social scientists should do to theories of morality?", "options": ["A) Make independent descriptions of cultures around the world.", "B) Strive to understand the basic differences between peoples.", "C) Appreciate the foundational value of the existing data.", "D) Carry out systematic field studies to gather new data."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （目的题） Carry out systematic field studies to gather new data.\n【定位】In future, if social scientists are serious about testing theories of morality, they will need to\ngather new data, more systematically, out in the field.\n今后，社会科学家若真要检验道德理论，就需要更系统地到实地去采集新数据。\n【信号】建议信号 will need to + 转折 rather than。Whitehouse 先说 should now set about\ntesting the theory in the field, rather than relying on old data，再用 they will need\nto gather new data, more systematically, out in the field 点明做法；need to 是建议信\n号，rather than relying on old data 则用对比把落点推向新数据。\n【替换】gather new data, more systematically, out in the field ↔\nCarry out systematic field studies to gather new data.\nsystematically 名词化、out in the field 转为 field studies\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nMake independent descriptions of cultures around the world. 搬了原文 These\ndescriptions were made independent of and prior to the theory we're testing 的原词\nindependent、descriptions，但那是在说本研究所依据的旧资料的来历，不是\nWhitehouse 主张今后该做的事，时间方向相反。\n借⽤原⽂：These descriptions were made independent of and prior to the theory we're testing\nB) 张冠李戴\nStrive to understand the basic differences between peoples. 借了第三段 Dr. Curry 那\n句 appreciation of what we have in common and how and why we differ 的差异话\n题，但那是 Curry 的期望，且题干问的是 Whitehouse 的主张，张冠李戴又跑了题。\n借⽤原⽂：appreciation of what we have in common and how and why we differ\nC) 与原文相反\nAppreciate the foundational value of the existing data. 里的 existing data 恰是\nWhitehouse 用 rather than relying on old data 明确否定的对象，他主张采集新数据，C\n取了被否定的旧数据一侧，方向反了。\n借⽤原⽂：rather than relying on old data", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the speaker say about our eyes?", "options": ["A) They might be the most important part of our eating experience.", "B) They can activate our brain functions in a most direct fashion.", "C) They can be viewed as the windows to our soul.", "D) They could mislead us in more ways than one."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They might be the most important part of our eating experience.\n【定位】For all the talk of the tongue, our eyes really might be the most important part of the\neating experience.\n尽管人们老在谈论舌头（的味觉），我们的眼睛或许才真正是吃这件事里最重要的部分。\n【信号】让步 For all… + 强调 really might。信号是句首让步结构 For all the talk of the tongue\n（\"尽管都在说舌头\"）后面才抛出真正主张，再加 our eyes really might be the most\nimportant part 里的 \"really\" 加重语气——让步之后、被强调的那句才是说话人的落点，\n正是答案句。\n【替换】our eyes really might be the most important part of the eating experience ↔\nThey might be the most important part of our eating experience.\n近乎原句、They 替 our eyes\n【排除】B) 偷换概念\nB 盗用了第 2 段原词 brain functions。原文说 A great number of our brain functions\nare dedicated to processing vision——是 \"很多脑功能用于处理视觉\"，主语是脑功能、\n用来佐证 eyes 重要；B 把它倒过来说成 \"眼睛激活脑功能\"，还加了原文没有的 \"in a most\ndirect fashion\"，是偷换概念，且没回答 \"说话人对 eyes 的核心论断\"。\n借⽤原⽂：A great number of our brain functions are dedicated to processing vision\nC) 未提及\nC 借用了关于眼睛的常见俗语 \"心灵的窗户\"，听着像常识、最易顺口选中，但整篇录音只把\n眼睛和 \"eating experience（吃的体验）\" 挂钩，从未说眼睛是 \"心灵的窗户\"，属原文未提\n及，凭常识选它就中招。\nD) 与原文相反\nD 说眼睛会 mislead us（误导我们），是贬义。但答案句对眼睛是高度肯定——our eyes\nreally might be the most important part of the eating experience，说话人在抬高眼睛\n而非贬低。D 的态度与原文相反，故排除。\n借⽤原⽂：our eyes really might be the most important part of the eating experience", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What does color actually do according to this lecture?", "options": ["A) It attracts food companies' growing attention.", "B) It adversely impacts one's eating experience.", "C) It invariably determines how food sells.", "D) It changes the way people taste food."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It changes the way people taste food.\n【定位】And the result is that color actually changes how we taste food.\n其结果就是，颜色实际上改变了我们品尝食物的方式。\n【信号】结果词 the result is + actually。信号是因果收束词 the result is that（\"其结果是\"）领出\n全段结论，又叠加 color actually changes how we taste food 里的 \"actually\"，与题干\n\"actually do\" 直接呼应——结果句 + actually 强调，这就是答案句。\n【替换】color actually changes how we taste food ↔ It changes the way people taste food.\nhow we taste = the way people taste\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nA 盗用第 1 段背景里的 \"food companies\"。原文确说 Food companies know that\nbetter color gives better prices，但那是讲公司在意颜色的背景，不是回答 \"color\nactually do（颜色实际起什么作用）\"；且原文是公司已经关注，A 说 \"growing attention\n（关注在增长）\" 也无依据，属答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：Food companies know that better color gives better prices\nB) 与原文相反\nB 借 eating experience 这个原文反复出现的词（第 2 段就有 the eating experience），\n却安上 \"adversely impacts（负面影响）\"。原文从头到尾把颜色讲成 \"改变味觉、帮助判\n断\" 的中性甚至有用的作用，从没说颜色 \"负面影响\" 吃的体验，态度相反，排除。\n借⽤原⽂：the most important part of the eating experience\nC) 过度推断\nC 盗用第 1 段 \"color is money / better prices\" 的卖钱话题，推成 It invariably\ndetermines how food sells.。原文只说 better color gives better prices（更好的颜色卖\n更好的价），是 \"有助于\"，C 用绝对词 \"invariably（无一例外地决定）\" 把话说死，超出原\n文，属过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：better color gives better prices", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does the brain use color to do?", "options": ["A) Enhance the taste.", "B) Make predictions.", "C) Identify distinct flavors.", "D) Enrich the eating experience."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Make predictions.\n【定位】The brain makes predictions using color.\n大脑利用颜色来做出预测。\n【信号】brain + color 同现的机制句。信号是录音第一次把 The brain 和 using color 放进同一\n句、直接给出动作的机制句 The brain makes predictions using color——题干问 \"大脑用\n颜色做什么\"，正是这句把主语、工具、动作三者一次说全，即答案句。\n【替换】The brain makes predictions using color ↔ Make predictions.\nmakes predictions = Make predictions\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 说 Enhance the taste.（增强味道），听着像 \"颜色让吃更好\" 的常识，但录音讲的是颜\n色让大脑做预测、形成对甜/酸的预期，从没说颜色 \"增强\" 味道本身，属原文未提及。\nC) 张冠李戴\nC 盗用第 3 段实验里的原词 \"identify the flavor\"。原文 each of them identified the\norange flavor 说的是 \"实验参与者借助看到颜色去辨认味道\"，是讲颜色影响味觉的证据；\n题问的是 \"大脑用颜色做什么\"，答案是 \"做预测\"，C 把实验现象当成大脑机制本身，属张冠\n李戴、答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：each of them identified the orange flavor\nD) 未提及\nD 说 Enrich the eating experience.（丰富吃的体验），借用了贯穿全篇的 \"eating\nexperience\" 话题词，但原文说大脑用颜色是去 \"做预测、形成味觉预期\"，从没说颜色用来\n\"丰富\" 吃的体验，属原文未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does the speaker say many of us search for throughout our lives?", "options": ["A) Social status.", "B) Financial resources.", "C) Meaning.", "D) Happiness."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Meaning.\n【定位】Searching for meaning is something many of us experience throughout our lives.\n追寻意义，是我们许多人一生中都会经历的事。\n【信号】开篇点题句即答案。录音第一句 Searching for meaning is something many of us\nexperience throughout our lives 与题干 throughout our lives 逐词呼应，是典型的‘开篇\n点题’信号——篇章独白常把核心对象放在第一句领出，这里把 meaning 摆在句首作主语，\n正是答案所在。\n【替换】Searching for meaning ↔ Meaning\n动名词短语取其宾语名词\n【排除】D) 原词陷阱\n录音紧接着说 having a sense of meaning can bring us happiness，happiness 在这里\n是‘意义带来的结果’，不是‘人们追寻的东西’。选项 Happiness 盗用了这个刚听到的原词，\n但答非所问——题目问的是 search for 的对象。\n借⽤原⽂：having a sense of meaning can bring us happiness\nB) 原词陷阱\nin the context of financial resources 里 financial resources 是这项研究的考察背景/变\n量，全篇没说它是‘人们一生追寻的东西’。选项 Financial resources 盗用了后文反复出现的\n财富一词来诱选。\n借⽤原⽂：in the context of financial resources\nA) 未提及\nFor some, this meaning is religious, some political and some interpersonal 列举的是\n意义的种类（宗教、政治、人际），通篇并未把 social status（社会地位）说成人们追寻的\n东西，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What do Rhia Catapano and colleagues find about economic resources?", "options": ["A) Their effect on people's happiness has long been overstated.", "B) Their influence on people's life varies with social contexts.", "C) They can affect people's experience of meaning.", "D) They can ensure people's overall well-being."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They can affect people's experience of meaning.\n【定位】Rhea Catapano from the University of Toronto and colleagues find meaning is a far weaker\npredictor of happiness for rich people than poorer people, suggesting economic resources can\nimpact how we experience meaning.\n多伦多大学的 Rhea Catapano 及其同事发现，对富人而言，意义对幸福的预测作用远弱于\n穷人，这表明经济资源会影响我们体验意义的方式。\n【信号】find…suggesting 引出结论。录音用 find meaning is a far weaker predictor of\nhappiness…suggesting economic resources can impact how we experience meaning\n把研究结论端出来：动词 find 直接对应题干的 find，而 suggesting 之后那半句正是对‘经\n济资源起什么作用’的定性回答，是结论信号最集中处。\n【替换】economic resources can impact how we experience meaning ↔\nThey can affect people's experience of meaning\nimpact=affect 同义替换\neconomic resources ↔ They\n代词回指经济资源\n【排除】D) 偷换概念\n录音说财富影响的是 how we experience meaning（体验意义的方式），并未说它能‘保证\n整体幸福’。选项 They can ensure people's overall well-being 借用了全篇高频词\nhappiness 的近义概念 well-being，还把‘影响’夸大成‘保证（ensure）’，对象与程度都改\n了。\n借⽤原⽂：economic resources can impact how we experience meaning\nA) 未提及\nmeaning is a far weaker predictor of happiness for rich people than poorer people\n谈的是‘穷富之间预测力强弱不同’，从未说‘财富对幸福的影响被长期夸大’。选项 Their\neffect on people's happiness has long been overstated 凭空造出一个‘被夸大’的评价，\n是无中生有，正应了预读对 overstated 这个程度词的预警。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音说差异取决于 income（收入高低），而非 social contexts（社会环境）。第三项研究\n里虽有 social ladder（社会阶梯）一词，但那是测量财富地位的工具，不是‘随社会环境而\n变’。选项 Their influence on people's life varies with social contexts 盗用了 social 这\n个原词来制造熟悉感。\n借⽤原⽂：they placed themselves on a \"social ladder\"", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What do we learn about Rhia Catapano and colleagues' second study?", "options": ["A) It used questions totally different from those in their first study.", "B) It focused on the sense of meaning of French participants.", "C) It analysed cases from a daily poll of US residents.", "D) It examined data collected from multiple countries."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It examined data collected from multiple countries.\n【定位】The second study looked at worldwide data.\n第二项研究考察的是全球范围的数据。\n【信号】序数词 second 直接定位。录音用序数词把三项研究串起来：first study…second study…\nfinal study。题干问 second study，就听 The second study looked at worldwide data\n这句——worldwide data（全球数据）是这项研究区别于前后两项的核心特征，序数词是\n最干净的定位信号。\n【替换】worldwide data ↔ data collected from multiple countries\nworldwide=multiple countries\n【排除】C) 张冠李戴\nThe first study used data from a daily poll of US residents 明说‘分析美国居民每日民调’\n的是第一项研究，不是第二项。选项 It analysed cases from a daily poll of US residents\n盗用了 daily poll of US residents 这串原话，但安到了错的研究上。\n借⽤原⽂：The first study used data from a daily poll of US residents\nB) 张冠李戴\nIn the final study, French participants indicated 表明‘聚焦法国被试’的是最终那项研究，\n而非第二项。选项 It focused on the sense of meaning of French participants 盗用\nFrench participants 这一原词，错配到第二项研究上。\n借⽤原⽂：In the final study, French participants indicated\nA) 与原文相反\n录音说第二项研究 Happiness was measured using the same questions as in the first\nstudy，幸福度用的是与第一项‘相同’的问题；选项 It used questions totally different\nfrom those in their first study 恰好把 same（相同）反说成 totally different（全然不\n同），正话反说。\n借⽤原⽂：Happiness was measured using the same questions as in the first study", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "Why do meaning and happiness have a weaker relationship in those with more resources?", "options": ["A) They might have more access to external sources of happiness.", "B) They might focus on an individual sense of satisfaction or meaning.", "C) They might be less easily affected by a community's overall feeling.", "D) They might be less adversely impacted by failure to achieve a purpose."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They might have more access to external sources of happiness.\n【定位】This might be the case because rich individuals have more access to other sources of\nhappiness—many of which will be external, like lack of stress or community.\n之所以如此，可能是因为富人能接触到更多其他的幸福来源——其中许多是外部的，比如没\n有压力、或拥有社群。\n【信号】because 引出原因。录音用 This might be the case because rich individuals have\nmore access to other sources of happiness 给出解释，because 是最直接的因果信号，\n其后那句正是‘富人为何关系更弱’的答案；后面 many of which will be external 进一步点\n明这些来源是‘外部的’。\n【替换】rich individuals have more access to other sources of happiness ↔\nThey might have more access to external sources of happiness\nnear-原词复现+补 external\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音末句 a focus on an internal sense of satisfaction, purpose or meaning is less\nimportant 说‘对内部满足/意义的关注变得不那么重要’，这是‘外部来源多’导致的结果，且\n方向是‘内部变次要’，并非‘富人聚焦内部满足’。选项 They might focus on an individual\nsense of satisfaction or meaning 盗用 internal/satisfaction/meaning 这串原词，却把\n因果与方向都说反了。\n借⽤原⽂：a focus on an internal sense of satisfaction, purpose or meaning is less important\nC) 偷换概念\n录音的 like lack of stress or community 把 community（社群）举例为‘外部幸福来源之\n一’，而选项 They might be less easily affected by a community's overall feeling 把\ncommunity 改造成‘群体的整体情绪’并说富人‘不易受其影响’，与原文‘社群是一种幸福来源’\n完全不是一回事。\n借⽤原⽂：like lack of stress or community\nD) 未提及\n录音通篇没说富人‘较少受目标未达成的负面打击’。选项 They might be less adversely\nimpacted by failure to achieve a purpose 借 purpose 一词（录音里 purpose 多次出\n现）拼出一个原文未提的因果，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appeal", "B) extravagantly", "C) intrinsically", "D) irony", "E) layman", "F) literally", "G) majestic", "H) malicious", "I) meadow", "J) mingle", "K) predator", "L) rage", "M) scrape", "N) shrewd", "O) thrifty"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) thrifty\n形容词\n【词性槽】Being ___ 是动名词作主语，空位须填形容词作表语\n【依据】全文主题是「省与花」，被评为美德的正是「节俭」\n结合 overview 与下一段 It is evil or sinful to spend little and to save much，作者反思\n的正是把「节俭/少花多存」当美德这件事；此处需要一个表「节俭」的形容词，thrifty 精\n准对应；它也与第 27 题的 extravagantly 形成全文核心对照。\n【竞争词】G) majestic 「雄伟的」与个人生活态度无关\nmajestic 多用于山河、宫殿等宏伟事物，描述人时也偏「庄严威严」，与「美德/生活习惯」的\n语义场不沾边。\nH) malicious 感情色彩反了——它是贬义\nmalicious 意为「心怀恶意的」，是贬义词；而后文紧跟 a virtue（美德），褒贬色彩冲突，直\n接排除。\nN) shrewd 「精明」更偏处世手腕，且第 34 题更需要它\nshrewd 指头脑精明、会算计，并不直接对应「省钱」这一道德范畴；而且本卷第 34 题 Those\nwho are __(34)__ and have got loans from the bank 在「精明地借贷投资房产」的语境里几\n乎只能由 shrewd 填上，先做易空的策略告诉我们 shrewd 要留给第 34 题。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 N) —— shrewd 也是形容词，且常被夸为美德。shrewd 既是形容\n词、又含「精明」这一常被视作褒义的特质，很容易被误填到 Being __(26)__ is no longer\na virtue to some people；防坑点：本卷主旨是「省 vs 花」，第 34 题更需要 shrewd，\n先看全文再回填，节俭（thrifty）才是和「a virtue」「spend little and save much」前后\n呼应的那个。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·Being ___ 是动名词作主语，空位"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appeal", "B) extravagantly", "C) intrinsically", "D) irony", "E) layman", "F) literally", "G) majestic", "H) malicious", "I) meadow", "J) mingle", "K) predator", "L) rage", "M) scrape", "N) shrewd", "O) thrifty"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) extravagantly\n副词\n【词性槽】动词 spend 后缺修饰语，要填副词\n【依据】与前文 luxuries、后文 accumulate 形成对照\n前一分句 some love luxuries 已立「奢华爱好」，下一分句 some accumulate as much\nas they can like a country mouse 立「拼命攒钱」；本空在「花钱方式」的维度上需要一\n个「挥霍地、毫无节制地」的副词，与「攒钱」形成强对比，extravagantly（奢侈地、挥\n霍地）正好对应。\n【竞争词】C) intrinsically 「本质上」与「花钱方式」无关\nintrinsically 描述「事物的内在属性」，搭配 spend 既不构成常见用法，也讲不通「本质地花\n钱」这种意思，与前后并列项的「花钱风格」维度对不上。\nF) literally 「字面意义上」无法与 spend 直接搭出对照\nliterally 用于强调「真的、确确实实」，常修饰带夸张色彩的动词短语，但此处需要的是「花钱\n的程度/方式」对照「攒钱」，literally spend 无法形成对比意义，方向偏离。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·动词 spend 后缺修饰语，要填副词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appeal", "B) extravagantly", "C) intrinsically", "D) irony", "E) layman", "F) literally", "G) majestic", "H) malicious", "I) meadow", "J) mingle", "K) predator", "L) rage", "M) scrape", "N) shrewd", "O) thrifty"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) appeal\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词性物主代词 its 后接名词\n【依据】lose one's appeal to sb.＝对某人失去吸引力\nlose one's appeal to sb. 是固定搭配；结合 As I was born poor and in the countryside,\nthe simple rural life seldom loses its __(28)__ to me——「简单的乡村生活对我从未失去\n吸引力」，语义自洽，搭配地道。\n【竞争词】D) irony 「讽刺」与「失去」搭配方向不对\nlose its irony 表「失去讽刺意味」，与「乡村生活对我」之间没有「讽刺」可言，且第 31 题\nthe irony is that... 是高度匹配 irony 的句型，应留给第 31 题。\nE) layman 「外行」是指人，不能作生活的属性\nlayman 指「门外汉」，是描述人的身份名词，不会被「乡村生活」拥有；且第 32 题 a ___ of\neconomics 是典型 a layman of 用法，应留给那里。\nI) meadow 「草地」是具体物，不能被「失去」\nmeadow 是具体名词（草地、牧场），代入 lose its meadow 完全讲不通——乡村生活不会\n「失去它的一块草地」。\nK) predator 「掠夺者」与本句感情色彩冲突\npredator 多含贬义「掠食者/剥削者」，与「乡村生活对我从未失去 ___」这种偏温情的语境冲\n突；且第 33 题 turn the borrower into the ___ rather than the decent and kind lender 明\n显需要它形成对照，应留给第 33 题。\nL) rage 「盛怒/风潮」与「乡村生活对我」不搭\nrage 作名词表「盛怒」或「时尚风潮」，lose its rage 不构成英文常见搭配，且语义与「乡村\n生活的吸引力」无关；第 30 题 in a rage to do 的固定结构更需要它。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词性物主代词 its 后接名词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appeal", "B) extravagantly", "C) intrinsically", "D) irony", "E) layman", "F) literally", "G) majestic", "H) malicious", "I) meadow", "J) mingle", "K) predator", "L) rage", "M) scrape", "N) shrewd", "O) thrifty"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) mingle\n动词\n【词性槽】have an opportunity to ___ 中 to 后接动词原形\n【依据】mingle with sb. 是「与人交往」的固定搭配\nmingle with the newly rich 即「与新富阶层打交道」，正是作者所说的 an experience\nwhich has revolutionized my view of lifestyle.——能改变其生活观的，只有「亲身接\n触」这件事，语义、搭配双双契合。\n【竞争词】M) scrape scrape 不与 with sb. 搭配「交往」\nscrape 作动词意为「刮、擦、勉强凑集」，常见搭配是 scrape by/along/a living，并不存在\nscrape with sb. 表示与人交往的用法；而且第 35 题 they have to __(35)__ a living 恰好是\nscrape a living 的经典搭配，先做易空策略提示要把 M 留给第 35 题。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·have an opportunity"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appeal", "B) extravagantly", "C) intrinsically", "D) irony", "E) layman", "F) literally", "G) majestic", "H) malicious", "I) meadow", "J) mingle", "K) predator", "L) rage", "M) scrape", "N) shrewd", "O) thrifty"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) rage\n名词\n【词性槽】be in a ___ to do sth. 中 a 后接名词\n【依据】in a rage to do＝狂热地、风靡地做某事\nrage 作名词除「盛怒」外，常指「时尚、风靡一时的事物」；in a rage to borrow and to\nspend 即「世界都疯狂地借钱、花钱」，与后文 with the USA leading the trend（trend\n也是同义复现）完全呼应。\n【竞争词】I) meadow 「草地」与「全世界都在借钱花钱」毫无关联\nmeadow 是具体名词且语义场完全脱节，代入后 in a meadow to borrow 不成句，立即排除。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 I) —— meadow 看似与 borrow/spend 无关却最容易被排除得太\n晚。in a meadow 听上去像「在一片草地上」，部分考生扫到 the whole world is in a ___\n时可能短暂迷惑；防坑点：be in a ___ to do sth. 是固定结构，后接表「热潮/狂热」的抽\n象名词，meadow 不在此类，看 to do 这条线就能立排。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be in a ___ to do st"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appeal", "B) extravagantly", "C) intrinsically", "D) irony", "E) layman", "F) literally", "G) majestic", "H) malicious", "I) meadow", "J) mingle", "K) predator", "L) rage", "M) scrape", "N) shrewd", "O) thrifty"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) irony\n名词\n【词性槽】定冠词 the 后＋is 前，须是名词作主语\n【依据】「最强最富却向别人借钱」恰是反讽\n前一句 The USA is still the richest and most powerful country in the world，紧接 but\nthe irony is that they borrow money from us——「最富最强反而要向我们借钱」正是\n「反讽」最典型的用法；the irony is that... 也是英语固定句型。\n【竞争词】E) layman 「外行」无法承担 the ___ is that... 的语义\nthe layman is that... 不成立，layman 是指人，无法被引出「事实」的句型使用。\nI) meadow 「草地」与 borrow money 无关\nmeadow 是具体名词，与「美国借钱」的话题完全脱节，语义不接。\nK) predator 「掠夺者」做主语后接 is that 不通\npredator 也是指人/动物的名词，the predator is that they borrow money from us 在意思上\n不通，且第 33 题 turn the borrower into the ___ 才是它的归宿。\nL) rage 「盛怒/风潮」不能承担 the ___ is that... 的「事实/反差」语义\nthe rage is that... 不构成英文中表达「事实/反讽」的句型；且第 30 题 in a rage to do 的固定\n结构更需要它。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·定冠词 the 后＋is 前，须是名词作"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appeal", "B) extravagantly", "C) intrinsically", "D) irony", "E) layman", "F) literally", "G) majestic", "H) malicious", "I) meadow", "J) mingle", "K) predator", "L) rage", "M) scrape", "N) shrewd", "O) thrifty"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) layman\n名词\n【词性槽】Though a ___ of economics 是名词性短语作让步状语\n【依据】a layman of economics＝经济学外行\n作者紧接说 at least I know that investing with borrowed money will turn the\nborrower into...——「虽是外行，但至少懂得……」，前后形成「让步＋让步内的肯定」逻\n辑；layman of 是「某领域的外行」的常见说法。\n【竞争词】I) meadow 「草地」不能修饰人的身份\nmeadow 不指人，与 Though I am a meadow of economics 完全不通，词义层面就排除。\nK) predator 「掠夺者」与作者自谦语气冲突\npredator 多用于贬义的「掠食者/掠夺者」，且后文紧跟 at least I know that... 这种「自谦但仍\n有见识」的口吻，搭配 predator 在语气和语义上都冲突；且第 33 题更需要它。\nL) rage 「盛怒/风潮」不能描述人在某学科的身份\nrage 作名词不指人，a rage of economics 不构成「某学科的人」这种用法；第 30 题 in a\nrage to do 才是它的归宿。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·Though a ___ of econ"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appeal", "B) extravagantly", "C) intrinsically", "D) irony", "E) layman", "F) literally", "G) majestic", "H) malicious", "I) meadow", "J) mingle", "K) predator", "L) rage", "M) scrape", "N) shrewd", "O) thrifty"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) predator\n名词\n【词性槽】turn A into the ___ rather than the decent and kind lender 中 the 后名词\n【依据】predator vs. lender＝掠夺者 vs. 仁慈的放贷人\nthe decent and kind lender 是「正派善良的放贷人」，与之对照、且语义反向的恰是\npredator（掠夺者/剥削者）；句意：「借钱投资会把借款人变成掠夺者，而非正派的放贷\n人」，与作者「借钱投资可致富」的论点完全一致。\n【竞争词】I) meadow 「草地」无法与「放贷人」并列对照\nmeadow 是具体名词，rather than 两端必须同类，meadow 与 lender 不同类，直接排除。\nL) rage 「盛怒」也不指人，无法与 lender 同类并列\nrage 作名词指「盛怒」或「风潮」，不指人，与 lender 不同类，无法承担 rather than 两端的\n对照；且第 30 题 in a rage to do 才是它的归宿。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·turn A into the ___"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appeal", "B) extravagantly", "C) intrinsically", "D) irony", "E) layman", "F) literally", "G) majestic", "H) malicious", "I) meadow", "J) mingle", "K) predator", "L) rage", "M) scrape", "N) shrewd", "O) thrifty"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) shrewd\n形容词\n【词性槽】Those who are ___ and have got loans 中 are 后＋and 并列谓语，空位须为形容词\n【依据】「精明」恰好描述会借贷投资房产致富的人\nshrewd 意为「精明的、有眼光的」，与下文 have got loans from the bank become\nrelatively rich by selling their purchased home at a price several folds higher. 描述的\n「从银行贷款 → 倒卖房产 → 致富」的精明操作完全契合。\n【竞争词】G) majestic 「雄伟」不能形容人的投资能力\nmajestic 多用于山川、宫殿等宏伟事物，做表语形容人时偏「庄严威严」，与「会贷款投资房\n产」的精明操作无关。\nH) malicious 贬义「恶意」与「合法投资致富」语境冲突\nmalicious 是「怀有恶意的」，但本句并未对借贷投资者作道德谴责，只是陈述「精明者→致\n富」的因果，贬义色彩不合适。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·Those who are ___ an"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appeal", "B) extravagantly", "C) intrinsically", "D) irony", "E) layman", "F) literally", "G) majestic", "H) malicious", "I) meadow", "J) mingle", "K) predator", "L) rage", "M) scrape", "N) shrewd", "O) thrifty"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) scrape\n动词\n【词性槽】they have to ___ a living 中 to 后接动词原形带宾语\n【依据】scrape a living＝勉强糊口的固定搭配\n上下文 Many of those poor parents who have the vision to send their children to\ncollege have benefited from this investment, even though they have to __(35)__ a\nliving. 说穷父母为送孩子上大学不惜「勉强糊口」，scrape a living 是六级常考的固定搭\n配，含义完全契合「贫困到只够维持生活」的语境。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·they have to ___ a l"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "According to a senior researcher, we will have secure food supply if we rid ourselves of ignorance about native crops.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】无知/native crops + senior researcher\n【定位】\"There is no food insecurity in the world, there is food ignorance,\" says Cecilia Tortajada, a\nsenior research fellow at the Institute of Water Policy at the National University of Singapore.\n\"Whenever we have native crops we tend to disregard them as if they were not valuable but\nthey are,\" she adds.\n「世界上不存在食物不安全，只存在食物无知，」 新加坡国立大学水政策研究所的资深研究\n员 Cecilia Tortajada 说。 「只要有本土作物，我们就倾向于无视它们，仿佛它们没有价\n值，但它们其实有价值，」 她补充说。\n【改写】a senior researcher ↔\na senior research fellow at the Institute of Water Policy at the National University of Singapore\n身份同位替换\nwe will have secure food supply if we rid ourselves of ignorance about native crops ↔\nThere is no food insecurity in the world, there is food ignorance\n否定对比 → 肯定因果重述\nnative crops ↔ native crops\n原词复现", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Most of the world's food supply comes from a tiny number out of thousands of crops that have been grown for centuries.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「a tiny number out of thousands」 的数量对比\n【定位】Prof Azam-Ali explains that just four crops—wheat, maize, rice and soybean—provide two-\nthirds of the world's food supply. \"We're dependent on these four,\" he says. \"But actually\nthere're 7,000 crops we've been farming for thousands of years. We ignore all of those.\"\nAzam-Ali 教授解释说，仅仅小麦、玉米、稻米和大豆这四种作物就提供了全世界三分之二\n的食物供应。 「我们依赖这四种作物，」 他说， 「但其实我们已经种植了几千年的作物有\n7000 种，我们对其他全都视而不见。」\n【改写】Most of the world's food supply ↔ two-thirds of the world's food supply\n分数 → 模糊量词\na tiny number out of thousands of crops ↔ just four crops\n具体小数 → 模糊小量词\nthat have been grown for centuries ↔ we've been farming for thousands of years\n时间跨度的上下位\n【辨邻】 D By 2050 it estimates the world must produce 50% more food to feed the\nprojected global population of 10 billion 讲的是 「未来要多生产多少」，而非\n「现在四种作物占多少」。\nBy 2050 it estimates the world must produce 50% more food", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "To provide their family with food when the staple crops failed, some African women farmers grew local crops in their backyards.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】African women + backyards + 主粮失败\n【定位】He came across alternative crops in the 1980s through the work of women farmers he met in\nNiger. The then-PhD student remembers marvelling at the crops they grew in their backyards,\nwithout the benefits of technology, to feed their families when the big crops failed.\n他在 1980 年代通过在尼日尔遇到的妇女农人的工作接触到了替代作物。这位当时还在读博\n的学生回忆，对她们在自家后院、不借助科技、在主要作物失败时种来养家的作物，他充满\n惊叹。\n【改写】African women farmers ↔ women farmers he met in Niger\n上位地理替换\ngrew local crops in their backyards ↔ the crops they grew in their backyards\n原词复现\nTo provide their family with food when the staple crops failed ↔\nto feed their families when the big crops failed\n同义动词与上下位词替换\n【辨邻】 I African women who grew bambara groundnut were actually punished for\ngrowing it 涉及的是 「殖民惩罚导致边缘化」 ，与 when the staple crops failed 这\n一动机条件无关。\nAfrican women who grew bambara groundnut were actually punished for growing it", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "High-yielding crops have occupied a dominant position in modern agriculture since the green revolution in the last century.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】high-yielding + green revolution + last century\n【定位】Ever since the \"green revolution\" of the 1960s, high-yielding crops have dominated\nmodern agriculture.\n自 1960 年代的 「绿色革命」 以来，高产作物一直主导着现代农业。\n【改写】High-yielding crops ↔ high-yielding crops\n原词复现\nhave occupied a dominant position in modern agriculture ↔ have dominated modern agriculture\n动词与同源名词短语的等值替换\nsince the green revolution in the last century ↔ Ever since the \"green revolution\" of the 1960s\n具体年代 → 上位时间段", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Growing alternative crops proved feasible in a variety of environments, but the critical question was whether they would be marketable.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「feasible in various environments」 + 「marketable」\n【定位】Project after project helped prove these crops were viable in different environments\nas alternatives to the staple ones. But the question of whether these crops would be\nmarketable remained.\n一个又一个项目帮助证明这些作物在不同环境中作为主粮的替代品是可行的。但它们是否能\n被市场接受这个问题仍然存在。\n【改写】Growing alternative crops proved feasible in a variety of environments ↔\nProject after project helped prove these crops were viable in different environments as\nalternatives to the staple ones\n形容词同义 + 名词同义\nbut the critical question was whether they would be marketable ↔\nBut the question of whether these crops would be marketable remained\n转折结构 + 关键词复现\n【辨邻】 E By investing in neglected local plants, countries can reduce their reliance on\nimported crops 是 「投资带来的好处」 的论述，没有 marketable 这一关键评估\n维度。\nBy investing in neglected local plants, countries can reduce their reliance on imported crops and\ntheir carbon-heavy supply chains", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "According to a professor, when the yields of staple crops fall, we will have to adapt to eating foods from alternative crops.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】professor + 主粮减产 + 改吃替代作物\n【定位】We \"have to get used to eating other crops\" as yields of staple crops fall, he says.\n他说，随着主粮作物的产量下降，我们 「必须习惯吃别的作物」 。\n【改写】According to a professor ↔ Tim Lang, professor of food policy at City, University of London\n身份具体化\nwhen the yields of staple crops fall ↔ as yields of staple crops fall\n连词同义替换\nwe will have to adapt to eating foods from alternative crops ↔\nhave to get used to eating other crops\n动词短语同义 + 名词同义\n【辨邻】 E warming climates threaten existing crops 后跟的是 「Bringing back the\nvariety」 的主动倡议，而非 will have to adapt to eating 这种 「不得不适应」 的被\n动结论。\nBringing back the variety of crops humans once ate also boosts food security at a time warming\nclimates threaten existing crops", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Urgent measures have to be taken to provide food for the projected world population without aggravating the climate.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】projected world population + 不加剧气候\n【定位】By 2050 it estimates the world must produce 50% more food to feed the projected global\npopulation of 10 billion. Meeting this demand without contributing to climate change calls for\nurgent solutions.\n据估计，到 2050 年，全世界必须多生产 50% 的食物，才能养活预计达到 100 亿的全球人\n口。在不加剧气候变化的前提下满足这一需求，需要紧急的解决方案。\n【改写】Urgent measures have to be taken ↔ calls for urgent solutions\n「呼吁紧急方案」 与 「必须采取紧急措施」 等值\nprovide food for the projected world population ↔\nto feed the projected global population of 10 billion\n动词同义 + 形容词原词复现\nwithout aggravating the climate ↔ without contributing to climate change\n动词短语近义改写\n【辨邻】 E Dietary diversification is critical to the future of humanity 是宣言式总结，缺\nprojected world population 与 Urgent measures 这两个具体要素。\nDietary diversification is critical to the future of humanity", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Colonial rule marginalised local crops by punishing Africans who grew them.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Colonial rule + punishing Africans + 种植\n【定位】\"African women who grew bambara groundnut were actually punished for growing it,\" says\nAzam-Ali. \"Colonial powers said you can't grow that because there's no oil. We can't get a\nmarket for it.\"\n「种植 bambara 花生的非洲妇女实际上因为种植它而受到惩罚，」 Azam-Ali 说。 「殖民\n列强说，你们不能种它，因为它不产油，我们没法给它打开市场。」\n【改写】Colonial rule ↔ Colonial powers\n近义名词替换\nmarginalised local crops ↔ can trace its marginalisation to colonial rule\n动词与同根名词的等值\nby punishing Africans who grew them ↔\nAfrican women who grew bambara groundnut were actually punished for growing it\n主动 → 被动 + 上下位人群\n【辨邻】 G the crops they grew in their backyards … to feed their families when the big\ncrops failed 描述的是 「主动求生式种植」 ，与 punishing Africans who grew\nthem 这一 「被惩罚」 的因果链相反。\nthe crops they grew in their backyards, without the benefits of technology, to feed their families\nwhen the big crops failed", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "As existing crops are endangered by global warming, we can increase food security by bringing back the many forgotten food crops.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】全球变暖威胁现有作物 + 恢复被遗忘作物\n【定位】Bringing back the variety of crops humans once ate also boosts food security at a time\nwarming climates threaten existing crops.\n在气候变暖威胁现有作物之际，恢复人类曾经食用的多样化作物，也能提升食物安全。\n【改写】As existing crops are endangered by global warming ↔\nat a time warming climates threaten existing crops\n条件连词与谓语同义替换\nwe can increase food security ↔ boosts food security\n动词同义替换\nby bringing back the many forgotten food crops ↔\nBringing back the variety of crops humans once ate\n近义名词替换\n【辨邻】 D Meeting this demand without contributing to climate change calls for urgent\nsolutions 提出问题，但没有 bringing back 这一具体对策。\nMeeting this demand without contributing to climate change calls for urgent solutions\nO have to get used to eating other crops 与 we can increase food security by\nbringing back the many forgotten food crops 在方向（被动适应 vs 主动恢复）与\n结论（习惯口味 vs 食物安全）上都不同。\nhave to get used to eating other crops", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Researchers are trying to find out how higher temperatures and CO₂ levels affect the nutritional composition of alternative crops.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】higher temperatures + CO₂ + nutritional composition\n【定位】It's one of multiple chambers where scientists are studying the impact of higher temperatures\nand carbon dioxide levels on the nutritional make-up of alternative crops.\n这是多个生长室之一，科学家们在那里研究更高温度与二氧化碳浓度对替代作物营养构成的\n影响。\n【改写】higher temperatures and CO₂ levels ↔ higher temperatures and carbon dioxide levels\n化学式与全称等值\nthe nutritional composition of alternative crops ↔ the nutritional make-up of alternative crops\n近义名词替换\nfind out how … affect … ↔ studying the impact of … on …\n句式重构\n【辨邻】 J growing carbon dioxide levels strip crops of their minerals 是结论式判断，与\nResearchers are trying to find out 这一研究行为不同。\ngrowing carbon dioxide levels strip crops of their minerals\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA 马来西亚一位水果农 Lim Kok Ann 因为市场小，只剩一棵 kedondong 树，转而种植\n市场更大的 longan 和菠萝；用一个真实农户的取舍引出 「被遗忘作物」 的困境。\n信息指纹 农户姓名与 kedondong 取舍\nB 在马来西亚乡村的三个银色穹顶里，全球研究中心 Crops For the Future (CFF) 把像\nkedondong 这样的 「替代」 作物加工成无糖果汁；引出 「被遗忘作物」 这一核心概\n念与 CFF。\n信息指纹 CFF 总部与无糖果汁\nC\nAzam-Ali 教授指出 just four crops—wheat, maize, rice and soybean—provide two-\nthirds of the world's food supply，而人类种植了几千年的 7,000 crops 大多被忽视。\n信息指纹 四种作物供两/三与七千种作物\nD 食品行业排放占全球温室气体近三分之一，到 2050 年要给 10 billion 人口多生产 50%\n食物，且不能加剧气候变化，因此 calls for urgent solutions。\n信息指纹 2050 年百亿人口与紧急解决方案\nE 在气候变暖威胁现有作物时，恢复 「被遗忘作物」 的多样性可以减少对进口作物的依\n赖、并 boosts food security；Azam-Ali 总结 Dietary diversification is critical to the\nfuture of humanity。\n信息指纹 气候变暖威胁下用多样性提升食物安全\nF\n食品安全专家 Cecilia Tortajada 提出 There is no food insecurity in the world, there is\nfood ignorance，认为人们对本土作物的无视才是问题。\n信息指纹 无知论与 Cecilia Tortajada\nG Azam-Ali 1980 年代在尼日尔看到 women farmers 在 backyards 种植替代作物，to\nfeed their families when the big crops failed，由此看到另立食物系统的机会。\n信息指纹 尼日尔后院妇女与主粮失败\nH Project after project helped prove these crops were viable in different environments\nas alternatives to the staple ones，但 the question of whether these crops would be\nmarketable remained；并介绍食品技术员 Tan Xinlin 用 moringa 替代部分小麦粉做蛋\n糕、把陌生原料 「现代化」 以打开市场。\n信息指纹 可行性已证而市场化存疑 + Tan Xinlin 配方\nI bambara groundnut 在非洲被边缘化要追溯到殖民统治：African women who grew\nbambara groundnut were actually punished for growing it；今天它是 CFF 评价最好\n的食品之一，并以 quinoa 为可借鉴的成功例子。\n信息指纹 殖民惩罚种植非洲妇女与 quinoa 案例\nJ Ever since the \"green revolution\" of the 1960s, high-yielding crops have dominated\nmodern agriculture，但现在 「营养正在成为定时炸弹」，应该投资本就更营养的被\n遗忘作物。\n信息指纹 1960 年代绿色革命与高产作物主导\nK 在 CFF 第三个穹顶里，科学家在多个生长室研究 the impact of higher temperatures\nand carbon dioxide levels on the nutritional make-up of alternative crops，确保 「未\n来作物」 在更暖气候下仍最健康。\n信息指纹 高温与 CO₂ 对替代作物营养构成的研究\nL\nFood Tank 主席 Danielle Nierenberg 指出 「被遗忘食物」 正形成全球势头，CFF 之外\n还有 Crop Trust、Slow Food、Icrisat 和 Bioversity International 等组织。\n信息指纹 全球势头与多家智库点名\nM\n尽管一些地方对被遗忘食物兴趣上升，但 the global spread of Western-style diets\nheavy in sugar, fat and processed foods 在更多地方仍占上风。\n信息指纹 西式饮食扩散压过被遗忘食物兴趣\nN 在马来西亚，推广本土作物的关键障碍是 the obsession with imported products；\nSitka 餐厅联合创始人 Jenifer Kuah 说顾客仍把外国食材当 status symbol。\n信息指纹 对进口产品的痴迷与 Sitka 餐厅\nO 城市大学伦敦食品政策教授 Tim Lang 认为气候变化将逼迫口味改变，We \"have to\nget used to eating other crops\" as yields of staple crops fall。\n信息指纹 Tim Lang 教授与主粮减产逼变口味", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "The alien finds the audience in the movie theatre clearly in a state of ______.", "options": ["A) total mindfulness", "B) extreme excitement", "C) spiritual elevation", "D) intense curiosity"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】The alien finds the audience in the movie theatre clearly in a state of ______ 问的是外\n星人眼中观众的客观状态，无 infer/imply/mainly 等抽象信号，是单点事实细节。\n【拆句】Q46 句子主干是 The alien finds the audience ... in a state of ______，find sb. in a state\nof ... 是「发现某人处于某种状态」的复合宾语结构；clearly 修饰整个判断，强调状态显而\n易见；in the movie theatre 是 audience 的后置定语，无陷阱。\n【定位】They are clearly in a state of high arousal. Their heart rate is elevated, they occasionally\nglance around nervously, and they sometimes jump collectively in their seats, and emit high-\npitched warning calls.\n他们显然处于一种高度唤起的状态。心率加快，偶尔紧张地四下张望，时不时在座位上一齐\n惊跳，并发出尖叫般的警示声。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\ntotal mindfulness 指「完全的正念/平静专注」，与 high arousal 描绘的生理躁动完全\n相反。\nB) ✓ 正确\nhigh arousal ↔ extreme excitement\nTheir heart rate is elevated ... jump collectively ... emit high-pitched warning calls ↔\nextreme excitement\n预判语义「高度兴奋」与 extreme excitement 在程度词 extreme=high、情绪词\nexcitement=arousal 两个维度上完全对位。\nC) 无中生有\nspiritual elevation 指「精神升华」，原文只描写心率 elevated 这种生理升高，未提及\n任何精神层面的升华。\nD) 偷换概念\nintense curiosity 把状态偷换成「强烈好奇」；但原文是因恐怖剧情引发的恐惧式高唤\n起，不是认知性好奇。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "Why do many people seek out scary entertainment, according to the author's research?", "options": ["A) They gain experience in overcoming horror in real life.", "B) They find joy in going through simulated horrible experiences.", "C) They have learned from hide-and-seek as kids the thrill involved.", "D) They have evolved to gain pleasure in escaping life-threatening situations."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】according to the author's research 把答案限制为作者研究中的一个具体结论，问的是事\n实层面的原因而非整体主旨，属细节题。\n【拆句】Q47 主句 Why do many people seek out scary entertainment 是标准 Why 一般疑问\n句；according to the author's research 是插入语，提示信源限定；seek out 是「主动去\n寻找」，与原文 voluntarily expose / seek out 同义，注意主动义。\n【定位】My research suggests that we humans evolved to find pleasure in situations that allow us to\nexperience negative emotions in a safe context.\n我的研究表明，人类在进化中学会了在能让我们于安全语境下体验负面情绪的情景中获得\n快感。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nThey gain experience in overcoming horror in real life 把「安全语境的模拟体验」\n偷换成「现实生活中克服恐怖」，与原文 safe context 矛盾。\nB) ✓ 正确\nfind pleasure ↔ find joy\nexperience negative emotions in a safe context ↔\ngoing through simulated horrible experiences\nThey find joy in going through simulated horrible experiences 两端都吻合预\n判：joy=pleasure、simulated horrible experiences=negative emotions in a\nsafe context。\nC) 以偏概全\nThey have learned from hide-and-seek as kids the thrill involved 拿一个例证 hide-\nand-seek 当原因，又把 pleasure 改成 thrill；hide-and-seek 是论据而非原因，本身\n不能解释成年人为何看恐怖片。\nD) 偷换概念\nThey have evolved to gain pleasure in escaping life-threatening situations 把「在\n安全语境下体验负面情绪」偷换成「在逃离致命威胁中获得快感」——原文是模拟、非\n真威胁；evolved、pleasure 借词制造伪等值。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What do children learn from hide-and-seek?", "options": ["A) How to avoid falling prey to an attacker.", "B) How to simulate a predator-prey interaction.", "C) How to keep themselves from catastrophic errors.", "D) How to turn a dangerous scenario into a safe one."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do children learn from hide-and-seek 问的是一个可在原文一句话里找到的具体收\n获，属典型细节题。\n【拆句】Q48 主干 What do children learn from hide-and-seek 是标准 wh- 疑问；learn from 是\n「从……中学到」，要求原文有相应的因果或收获句，无陷阱。\n【定位】This simulation gives the child crucial information about how to avoid becoming prey, and\nchildren tend to find that kind of activity deeply satisfying, presumably because it gives them\na safe experience of a potentially catastrophic scenario.\n这种模拟向孩子提供了关于「如何避免成为猎物」的关键信息，孩子往往觉得这种活动极其\n令人满足，大概是因为它让他们在安全条件下体验了一种潜在的灾难性情境。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nhow to avoid becoming prey ↔ How to avoid falling prey to an attacker\nThe kid hides and the adult pretends to be a predator ↔ an attacker\nHow to avoid falling prey to an attacker 是预判的近乎逐字改写，avoid 对 avoid、\nfalling prey 对 becoming prey、attacker 对 predator。\nB) 答非所问\nHow to simulate a predator-prey interaction 学的是「如何模拟捕食情境」，但原文\n说「这场模拟（this simulation）」是大人创造的，孩子从中学到的是避险知识，而不\n是模拟技巧本身。\nC) 无中生有\nHow to keep themselves from catastrophic errors 凭空造出「灾难性错误」一说；\n原文只有 catastrophic scenario「灾难性情境」，且讨论的是体验该情境而非「避免\n错误」。\nD) 偷换概念\nHow to turn a dangerous scenario into a safe one 把原文「在安全条件下体验潜在\n的灾难性情境」偷换成「把危险情境变安全」——原文的 safe 是体验时的环境条件，并\n非孩子把危险情境改造成了安全情境。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "Why is horror gratifying to many people?", "options": ["A) It reminds them of an ancient set of biological defence mechanisms.", "B) It triggers their imagination to travel into dangerous virtual worlds.", "C) It allows them to learn what fear feels like and how to tackle it.", "D) It activates their evolved fear system and their unique fantasy."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】Why is horror gratifying to many people 问的是可在一句原文中找到的因果细节，作者\n给出明确的 because 句，非整体主旨。\n【拆句】Q49 主干 Why is horror gratifying to many people 是 be + adj. 的因果疑问句；\ngratifying 是现在分词作表语，等同 pleasing，要在原文找其同义表达 pleasurable，无结\n构陷阱。\n【定位】Horror is pleasurable to many people because it lets us play with negative emotions and\ndevelop coping strategies. We learn what it feels like to be truly afraid, and we learn how to\nhandle negative emotion.\n对许多人而言，恐怖之所以令人愉悦，是因为它让我们演练负面情绪并发展出应对策略。我\n们由此了解真正害怕的感觉，也学会如何处理负面情绪。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nIt reminds them of an ancient set of biological defence mechanisms 把「让我们演\n练负面情绪」偷换成「让我们想起远古防御机制」——原文是「利用」(exploiting) 防\n御机制，而不是「使我们回忆起」。\nB) 答非所问\nIt triggers their imagination to travel into dangerous virtual worlds 描述的是恐怖\n如何作用（机制），而非「为什么令人满足」（原因）；机制 vs 原因不同问，且 P12\n的话题是 how，不是 why。\nC) ✓ 正确\nWe learn what it feels like to be truly afraid ↔ learn what fear feels like\ndevelop coping strategies ... learn how to handle negative emotion ↔ how to tackle it\nlearn what fear feels like and how to tackle it 用并列结构同时覆盖预判的两层「体\n验」和「应对」，是原文最完整的同义改写。\nD) 答非所问\nIt activates their evolved fear system and their unique fantasy 是把 P12 机制段的\n两个核心要素「evolved fear system」与「imagination」拼起来，依旧回答的是「机\n制」而非「为什么令人满足」。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What will one experience if they watch a horror film through without being hurt?", "options": ["A) A strong sense of clear relevance.", "B) A profound sense of good fortune.", "C) A profound sense of intense relief.", "D) A strong sense of being in control."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What will one experience 询问的是一段可被定位的具体心理体验，原文末段以 you'll\nprobably experience 直接给出，按细节题处理。\n【拆句】Q50 主干 What will one experience 是宾语前置的 wh- 疑问；if they watch a horror\nfilm through without being hurt 是条件状语从句，through 是副词「自始至终」，\nwithout being hurt 是被动动名词作伴随状语；they 指代 one，无指代陷阱。\n【定位】If you make it through the film in one piece, you'll probably experience a strong sense of\nmastery, a sense that you were able to make it through an appalling experience.\n如果你能毫发无损地撑过这部电影，你大概会体验到一种强烈的掌控感——一种自己能挺过\n令人毛骨悚然之体验的胜任感。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nA strong sense of clear relevance 摘取原文末句 critically relevant 中 relevant 的衍\n生形态，把后置的 critically relevant 偷换为 clear relevance；原文是说「处理恐惧的\n能力未来可能会变得至关重要」，并非「看完后体验到清晰的相关感」。\nB) 无中生有\nA profound sense of good fortune 描述「幸运感」，原文从未提及；作者强调的是因\n撑过而获得的胜任感，而非「庆幸自己运气好」。\nC) 无中生有\nA profound sense of intense relief 描述「释然感」，原文没有任何「relief/放松」语\n义；mastery 强调的是控场，而非紧张消退。\nD) ✓ 正确\na strong sense of mastery ↔ A strong sense of being in control\na sense that you were able to make it through an appalling experience ↔ being in control\nstrong sense of 对原文原词，mastery 与 being in control 是同义场最佳对应，D 字\n面命中预判。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What do we learn about beauty in the digital arena?", "options": ["A) It dictates the taste of digital media.", "B) It has been in the making for years.", "C) It has ushered in a new awakening.", "D) It is defined by social media."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn about beauty in the digital arena 是问\"关于 X 我们了解到什\n么\",指向原文对 X 的客观陈述。\n【拆句】Q51 是一个简单的 wh-疑问句,主干 What do we learn (about X),about 短语作 learn 的\n状语,整句无从句、无否定,无理解陷阱。\n【定位】But in the digital arena, social media determines what is considered beautiful.\n但在数字领域,是社交媒体决定了什么才被认为是美的。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIt dictates the taste of digital media 让\"美\"反过来去 dictate digital media,原文是\nsocial media 在 determine beauty,主客被掉换。\nB) 张冠李戴\nIt has been in the making for years 谈的是\"已经酝酿多年\",原文只说 dictated to us\nfor years 的是\"physical world 的旧美标准\",并非 beauty in the digital arena 本身。\nC) 张冠李戴\nIt has ushered in a new awakening 说\"数字领域的美开启了新觉醒\",但原文 An\nawakening has been taking place in the physical world 明确把觉醒放在 physical\nworld,而非 digital arena。\nD) ✓ 正确\nsocial media determines what is considered beautiful ↔ It is defined by social media\nIt is defined by social media 几乎就是预判句\"美由社交媒体决定\"的英文被动版,正中定\n位句 social media determines what is considered beautiful 的核心信息。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does the passage say about beauty in the physical world?", "options": ["A) Women are under constant pressure to keep up with beauty models.", "B) Women are encouraged to pursue a beauty ideal that has never existed.", "C) A fight is going on to remove pressure on women to conform to an absurd beauty ideal.", "D) Media outlets have begun to present as beauty models trendy women without any makeup."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the passage say about beauty in the physical world 直接问\"文章对 X 怎么\n说\",取的是原文事实陈述。\n【拆句】Q52 主干 What does the passage say (about X),about 短语后置作 say 的状语,无从句、\n无否定,理解无障碍。\n【定位】In the physical world, the struggle goes against the underlying pressure exerted on women to\nconform to an unrealistic beauty ideal.\n在现实世界里,这场斗争所反对的是施加在女性身上、要求她们顺从一种不切实际的美标准的\n潜在压力。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nWomen are under constant pressure to keep up with beauty models 强调女性\"持\n续被施压\",方向恰好与原文的\"对抗这种压力\"相反,文章重点在抗争,而非顺从。\nB) 无中生有\nWomen are encouraged to pursue a beauty ideal that has never existed 中的\nencouraged to pursue 在原文找不到对应——原文谈的是被施压,而非被鼓励;\"never\nexisted\"也并非原文 unrealistic 的等值表达。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe struggle goes against the underlying pressure exerted on women to conform to an unrealistic\nbeauty ideal\n↔ A fight is going on to remove pressure on women to conform to an absurd beauty ideal\nA fight is going on to remove pressure on women to conform to an absurd\nbeauty ideal 把\"正在进行抗争\"+\"对象是施加在女性身上的、要她们顺从荒谬美标准的\n压力\"两层全保留,正是预判内容。\nD) 偷换概念\nMedia outlets have begun to present as beauty models trendy women without\nany makeup 把媒体改变形象人选(那些\"不符合所谓理想身材\"的女性)与 influencers 不\n化妆自拍混在一起,且原文是 influencers 上传素颜照,不是 media outlets 把\"时尚的不\n化妆女性\"包装成 beauty models。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about the Instagram face?", "options": ["A) It is now regarded as the new beauty ideal.", "B) It is what most women will go after in 2050.", "C) It is being much talked about on social media.", "D) It is a perfect illustration of the ultimate beauty."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the passage about the Instagram face 仍是问对 X 这一具体对\n象的客观陈述。\n【拆句】Q53 主干 What do we learn (from the passage) (about X),两个介词短语都作状语,无从\n句、无陷阱。\n【定位】Today, however, the Instagram face has become the new beauty ideal.\n然而,如今 Instagram face 已经成为新的美标准。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe Instagram face has become the new beauty ideal ↔\nIt is now regarded as the new beauty ideal\nIt is now regarded as the new beauty ideal 几乎是定位句的字面同义改写,锁定\n即可。\nB) 张冠李戴\nIt is what most women will go after in 2050 把\"2050\"这个时间安在 Instagram face\n头上,而 the popularity of the cyborg look, which illustrates what Americans will\nlook like in 2050 里 2050 描述的是 cyborg look,而非 Instagram face。\nC) 无中生有\nIt is being much talked about on social media 加入了\"正在被大量讨论\"这层意思,原\n文只说它\"成了新美标准\",并未提及讨论度。\nD) 过度推断\nIt is a perfect illustration of the ultimate beauty 用 perfect 与 ultimate 两个最高程\n度词,原文只说 the new beauty ideal,既无\"完美\"也无\"终极\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What has obsessive filtering resulted in?", "options": ["A) A good many women striving to reach an impossible level of perfection.", "B) An urge to turn the entire game played on the network to one's advantage.", "C) A tendency to regard one's body as an object of observation and judgment.", "D) An increasing number of women filtering their images before uploading them."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What has obsessive filtering resulted in 问的是某行为带来的具体后果,在原文中必有对应\n陈述。\n【拆句】Q54 是简单 wh-疑问句,现在完成时 has resulted in 强调动作的延续后果,主语 obsessive\nfiltering 是名词化结构,理解上把它读作\"反复地过度滤镜化\"即可。\n【定位】One of the significant consequences of obsessive filtering is the emerging tendency to treat\noneself as an object to be observed and valued, in the same way another person observes and\njudges from the side.\n过度滤镜化的一个显著后果是,人们正在出现一种倾向:把自己当作一个被观察、被评价的客\n体,就好像旁人从一旁观察并评判一样。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nA good many women striving to reach an impossible level of perfection\n中\"impossible level of perfection\"是 P8 与 P9 关于滤镜带来的整体后果,不是 P7 这句\n关于 obsessive filtering 的 consequence。\nB) 无中生有\nAn urge to turn the entire game played on the network to one's advantage 的\"为\n己所用\"是原文完全没有的概念,只是借用了 the entire game played on the networks\n的字面壳。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe emerging tendency to treat oneself as an object to be observed and valued ↔\nA tendency to regard one's body as an object of observation and judgment\nA tendency to regard one's body as an object of observation and judgment 把\"被\n观察与被评价的客体\"这层意思以名词化方式呈现,与预判完全一致。\nD) 答非所问\nAn increasing number of women filtering their images before uploading them 描\n述的就是 obsessive filtering 这一行为本身(原因),而非 resulted in 所问的后果。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author want to emphasize at the end of the passage regarding Americans' obsession with looks?", "options": ["A) Cosmetic surgery should be made more accessible to the masses.", "B) Psychological intervention should be introduced to alleviate it.", "C) Their time spent on social media should be strictly controlled.", "D) Its root cause should be meticulously examined and analyzed."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度题）\n【判型】What does the author want to emphasize at the end of the passage 关心的是作者\"想\n强调什么\",指向作者的主观倾向与建议。\n【拆句】Q55 主干 What does the author want to emphasize,后接 at the end of the\npassage(位置状语)和 regarding X(关于 X 的话题状语),两段状语只是补充语境,核心动词\n是 emphasize。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nCosmetic surgery should be made more accessible to the masses 主张更易获得整\n容,但原文 not cosmetic intervention 已经明确排除\"美容干预\"。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthe only thing that can fix it is not cosmetic intervention, but mental health care ↔\nPsychological intervention should be introduced to alleviate it\ncosmetic intervention ↔ Psychological intervention\nPsychological intervention should be introduced to alleviate it 把作者真正强调的\nmental health care 改成同义表达 psychological intervention,正中作者立场。\nC) 过度推断\nTheir time spent on social media should be strictly controlled 提出严格控制使用时\n间,而原文 The time spent on social media creates an urge… 只把\"花在社媒的时\n间\"作为成因列出,并未呼吁去 strictly control 它。\nD) 无中生有\nIts root cause should be meticulously examined and analyzed 主张\"细致检查与分\n析其根本原因\",但原文只给方案(mental health care),并未呼吁去\"分析根因\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "态度题"]}]});
